/* Search page data built into the app.
 * Nosework Regs and Buried Hide Regs: rebuilt by Claude when a new version is issued.
 * Beyond the Sniff: only a fallback – the app downloads the latest from the Google Doc (see reference-sync.js). */
window.REFERENCE = {"regs":{"versions":{"r":"Aug 2026","b":"May 2026"},"sections":[{"d":"r","g":"Definition of Nosework","t":"SSANZ Definition of Nosework","h":"<p>Nosework is a sport that allows dogs to use their natural olfactory abilities and to work in partnership with their handler to locate specific target odours.</p><p>Nosework is an uninterrupted performance by a dog and handler to demonstrate working as a team and can be easily performed in a wide variety of locations.</p><p>In a SSANZ trial each team works together to locate and communicate that a hide/hides has/have been found.</p><p>The arrangement of the searches in Nosework are modelled on those of traditional working detection dogs. The overall performance should convey an image of fun, enthusiasm, and highlight how naturally and effectively the team works together.</p><p>Our aim is to provide a safe and enriching environment that promotes a trusting and lasting relationship. Harsh handling will not be tolerated and may result in immediate disqualification with the handler not permitted to participate any further on that day.</p><p>The primary focus of the trial is to achieve a qualifying score. Performance/competitive targets may be included at the discretion of the Organisers when providing prizes and awards.</p>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.1 Novice Nosework Title (NWNov)","h":"<p>Objective for Novice is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly locate a single hide in four different search areas within the allotted time.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Novice Nosework Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) Dog must attain two SSANZ Novice Qualifying Trial certificates to achieve their Novice title. Criteria outlined in 1.2.</li><li>c) A dog can continue trialling in Novice until they have accumulated a maximum of five SSANZ Novice Qualifying Trial certificates.</li><li>d) A dog that earns five SSANZ Novice Qualifying Trial certificates will receive the Super Star Award.</li><li>e) An exception to 1.1(c) is when a dog with four qualifying certificates is entered on both days at a double trial and qualifies on the first day. In this instance they are permitted a sixth certificate if successful on the second day.</li><li>f) Once a dog has a qualifying certificate at the Intermediate level, the dog can no longer trial at Novice level.</li><li>g) An exception to 1.1 (f) is when a dog is entered on both days of a double trial in Novice and Intermediate. If the team qualifies in Intermediate on the first day, they will be permitted to trial in Novice on the second day.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.2 Novice Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) Complete all four Novice searches, at a single trial, in the respective allocated times and correctly locate all hides.</li><li>b) Each search element is worth 25 points.</li><li>c) A minimum score of 90 out of a possible 100 points over the four searches is required to achieve a qualifying certificate.</li><li>d) One incorrect alert call is permitted in total over the four Novice searches. Once a \"false, continue\" is called by the Judge, the hide still needs to be located and a correct alert called.</li><li>e) The dog must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.3 Intermediate Nosework Title (NWInt)","h":"<p>Objective for Intermediate is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly locate a single hide in four different search areas within the allotted time.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Intermediate Nosework Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) Dog must attain two SSANZ Intermediate Qualifying Trial certificates under a minimum of 2 Judges at 2 different locations. Criteria outlined in 1.4.</li><li>c) A dog can continue trialling in Intermediate until they have accumulated a maximum of five SSANZ Intermediate Qualifying Trial certificates.</li><li>d) A dog that earns five SSANZ Intermediate Qualifying Trial certificates will receive the Super Star Award.</li><li>e) An exception to 1.3(c) is when a dog with four qualifying certificates is entered on both days at a double trial, and qualifies on the first day, they are permitted a sixth certificate if successful on the second day.</li><li>f) Once a dog has a qualifying certificate at Advanced, the dog can no longer trial at Intermediate.</li><li>g) An exception to 1.3 (f) is when a dog is entered on both days of a double trial in Intermediate and Advanced. If the team qualifies in Advanced on the first day, they will be permitted to trial in Intermediate on the second day.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.4 Intermediate Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) Complete all four Intermediate searches at a single trial in the respective allocated times and correctly locate all hides.</li><li>b) Each search element is worth 25 points.</li><li>c) A minimum score of 90 points out of a possible 100 points over the four searches is required to achieve a qualifying certificate.</li><li>d) There may be no incorrect alert calls over the four Intermediate searches.</li><li>e) The dog must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.5 Advanced Nosework Title (NWAdv)","h":"<p>Objective for Advanced is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly locate two hides in each of four different search areas.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Advanced Nosework Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) Dog must attain two SSANZ Advanced Qualifying Trial certificates under a minimum of 2 different Judges at 2 different locations. Criteria outlined in 1.6.</li><li>c) A dog can continue trialling in Advanced until they have accumulated a maximum of five SSANZ Advanced Qualifying Trial certificates.</li><li>d) A dog that earns five SSANZ Advanced Qualifying Trial certificates will receive the Super Star Award.</li><li>e) An exception to 1.5(c) is when a dog with four qualifying certificates is entered on both days at a double trial and qualifies on the first day. In this case they are permitted a sixth certificate if successful on the second day.</li><li>f) Once a dog has a qualifying certificate at Excellent, the dog can no longer trial at Advanced.</li><li>g) An exception to 1.5 (f) is when a dog is entered on both days of a double trial in Advanced and Excellent. If the team qualifies in Excellent on the first day, they will be permitted to trial in Advanced on the second day.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.6 Advanced Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) Complete all four Advanced searches at a single trial in the respective allocated time and correctly locate all hides.</li><li>b) Each search element is worth 25 points. Each hide/odour source has equal value.</li><li>c) A minimum score of 90 out of a possible 100 points over the four searches is required to achieve a qualifying certificate.</li><li>d) There may be no incorrect alert calls over the four Advanced searches.</li><li>e) The dog must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.7 Excellent Nosework Title (NWExc)","h":"<p>Objective for Excellent is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly locate 12 hides in total over five different search areas. The five search areas will be one each of container, vehicle, interior, and exterior, and a fifth area selected by the organisation from those four elements. Three different odours may be used as described in the Excellent Criteria section.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Excellent Nosework Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) Dog must attain two SSANZ Excellent Qualifying Trial certificates under a minimum of two different Judges at two different locations. Criteria outlined in 1.8.</li><li>c) A dog can continue trialling in Excellent until they have accumulated a maximum of five SSANZ Excellent Qualifying Trial certificates.</li><li>d) If a dog receives five SSANZ Excellent Qualifying Trial certificates they will receive the Super Star Award.</li><li>e) An exception to 1.7(c) is when a dog with four qualifying certificates is entered on both days at a double trial and qualifies on the first day. In this case they are permitted a sixth certificate if successful on the second day.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.8 Excellent Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) Complete all five Excellent searches at a single trial in the respective allocated time and correctly locate all hides.</li><li>b) Each hide will be allocated 10 points.</li><li>c) A minimum score of 111 out of a possible 120 points over the five searches is required to achieve a qualifying certificate.</li><li>d) A dog can continue trialling in Excellent until they have accumulated a maximum of five SSANZ Excellent Qualifying Trial certificates.</li><li>e) There may be no incorrect alert calls over the five Excellent searches.</li><li>f) The dog must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.9 Premium Nosework Title (NWPre)","h":"<p>Objective for Premium is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly locate 15 hides in total over six different search areas. The six search areas will be one each of container, vehicle, interior, and exterior, and two more areas selected by the organisation from those four elements. The two additional areas must be different from each other. Three different odours may be used as described in the Premium Criteria section.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Premium Nosework Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) Dog must attain two SSANZ Premium Qualifying Trial certificates under a minimum of two different judges at two different locations. Criteria outlined in 1.10.</li><li>c) If a dog receives five SSANZ Premium Qualifying Trial certificates they will receive the Super Star Award.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"1 Level Requirements – Awards","t":"1.10 Premium Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) Complete all six Premium searches at a single trial in the respective allocated time and correctly locate all the hides.</li><li>b) Each hide will be allocated 10 points.</li><li>c) A minimum score of 141 out of a possible 150 points over the six searches is required to achieve a qualifying certificate.</li><li>d) A dog can continue trialling in Premium until they have accumulated a maximum of five SSANZ Premium Qualifying Trial certificates.</li><li>e) There may be no incorrect alert calls over the six Premium searches.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"2 Set Up of Search Areas – General","t":"2.1–2.10 Hides and search areas","h":"<ul><li>2.1 Two odour-infused Q-Tips are to be placed inside either a plastic, rubber, silicone, or metal vessel and this shall be known as the hide.</li><li>2.2 Hide vessels will have a maximum capacity of 50ml with adequate ventilation to allow the odour to be released into the area.</li><li>2.3 Hides must be placed in a manner or secured to a surface such that they cannot be easily displaced or dislodged by the dog. A hide is to be secured inside a container in a manner that if the container moves the hide will not move or make a sound.</li><li>2.4 The hide will be placed by the Judge or designated official a minimum of 15 minutes before the first team commences searching.</li><li>2.5 The hide must not be visible to the team in the normal course of the search.</li><li>2.6 The hide should be placed in such a way that it is not at risk of ingestion by the dog.</li><li>2.7 Additional vessels must be available in case of contamination or visible damage.</li><li>2.8 The search area should be clean and safe.</li><li>2.9 The search area will be clearly defined by boundary markers (flags, cones, rope, pegs, tape, or natural visual landmarks), structures, or walls.</li><li>2.10 There must be a clearly defined start line.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"2 Set Up of Search Areas – General","t":"2.11 Container Search – General","h":"<ul><li>a) All containers used in this search must be clean, and \"cold\" containers must never have been used as \"hot\" ones or come in contact with target odour. i) No contamination from foodstuffs or strong-smelling substances. ii) Containers must be closed but have adequate holes or visible ventilation points for the odour to escape. Vents shall be no wider than 2.5 centimetres (25mm). iii) This search may be conducted indoors or outdoors.</li><li>b) The start line should be no less than one metre from the first container.</li><li>c) Additional hot containers and cold containers will be on standby in case any need to be replaced, and these should be stored away from the search area.</li><li>d) Distractions are items intentionally placed in a container to test the dog's ability to ignore irrelevant stimuli and remain focused on locating the target odour. i) Distractions Non-food: Any item that is not intended for consumption by a person or dog. ii) Distraction Food: Any item that a dog or person can safely eat. iii) Distractions not permitted - Kong, gun oil, droppings, poisons, any item containing essential oils, any item that is perishable, rotten, dead (e.g. animal, birds, fish), anything toxic to a dog, any highly volatile substances.</li><li>e) Also refer to Set Up of Search Areas - level specific.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"2 Set Up of Search Areas – General","t":"2.12 Interior Search – General","h":"<ul><li>a) This search will be conducted inside a building or room.</li><li>b) This area may be a whole room or defined section within a larger room.</li><li>c) The interior can be as it was found or staged at the Judge's discretion.</li><li>d) Also refer to Set Up of Search Areas - level specific.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"2 Set Up of Search Areas – General","t":"2.13 Exterior Search – General","h":"<ul><li>a) Exterior areas (refer to definitions) should be outdoors and may be covered, in the case of a deck or patio.</li><li>b) The exterior area can be as it was found or staged at the Judge's discretion.</li><li>c) Also refer to Set Up of Search Areas - level specific.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"2 Set Up of Search Areas – General","t":"2.14 Vehicle Search – General","h":"<ul><li>a) The first vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>b) There must be clear access (minimum of 2 metres) to all sides of the vehicle.</li><li>c) Hides must not be inside any vehicle.</li><li>d) Refer to Set Up of Search Areas for level-specific criteria.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"3 Novice Search Criteria","t":"3.1 Novice – General","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) One hide per search element.</li><li>b) Oil of Clove Bud (Syzygium aromaticum syn. Eugenia caryophyllata) is the odour used in SSANZ Novice trials.</li><li>c) No formal indication or TFR (Trained Final Response) is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>d) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"3 Novice Search Criteria","t":"3.2 Novice Container search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 2 minutes.</li><li>b) May be conducted indoors or outdoors.</li><li>c) 10-15 containers set in a geometric shape.</li><li>d) Containers must all be made from the same material and may be either cardboard or plastic.</li><li>e) Containers must remain separate — do not place containers inside other containers.</li><li>f) The containers will be uniform size with a minimum width of 210mm and maximum width of 600mm.</li><li>g) The containers shall be no more than 400mm in height.</li><li>h) All containers are placed at ground level.</li><li>i) The start line should be no less than one metre from the first container.</li><li>j) There is to be a minimum distance of 750mm and a maximum distance of 1500mm between containers. If the containers are in rows there is to be a minimum distance of 900mm and a maximum distance of 1500 between rows.</li><li>k) Containers can be weighted to reduce accidental movement by the dog.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"3 Novice Search Criteria","t":"3.3 Novice Interior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 3 minutes.</li><li>b) Search area will be between 60 and 100 sqm.</li><li>c) Hide maximum height 650mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"3 Novice Search Criteria","t":"3.4 Novice Exterior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 3 minutes.</li><li>b) Search area will be between 75 and 200 sqm.</li><li>c) Hide maximum height 650mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"3 Novice Search Criteria","t":"3.5 Novice Vehicle search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 3 minutes.</li><li>b) One passenger vehicle, either a car, ute, or minivan.</li><li>c) The first vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>d) Clear access to all sides (minimum of 2 metres from trees, vegetation, objects, walls or boundaries).</li><li>e) Hide maximum height 650mm.</li><li>f) Hide maximum depth under vehicle 100mm.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"4 Intermediate Search Criteria","t":"4 Intermediate – General","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) One hide per search element.</li><li>b) Oil of Sweet Birch (Betula lenta), is the odour used in SSANZ Intermediate trials.</li><li>c) No formal indication or TFR is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>d) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"4 Intermediate Search Criteria","t":"4.1 Intermediate Container search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 2 minutes.</li><li>b) May be conducted indoors or outdoors.</li><li>c) 12-18 containers set in a geometric shape.</li><li>d) Containers must be random size and be either cardboard or plastic. All containers must be made from the same material.</li><li>e) Containers must remain separate — do not place containers inside other containers.</li><li>f) The containers shall be no more than 400mm in height.</li><li>g) All containers are placed at ground level.</li><li>h) The start line should be no less than one metre from the first container.</li><li>i) There is to be a minimum distance of 750mm and a maximum distance of 1500mm between containers. If the containers are in rows there is to be a minimum distance of 900mm and a maximum distance of 1500 between rows.</li><li>j) Containers can be weighted to reduce accidental movement by the dog.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"4 Intermediate Search Criteria","t":"4.2 Intermediate Interior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 3 minutes.</li><li>b) Search area will be between 60 and 140 sqm.</li><li>c) Hide maximum height 650mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"4 Intermediate Search Criteria","t":"4.3 Intermediate Exterior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 4 minutes.</li><li>b) Search area will be between 100 and 300 sqm.</li><li>c) Hide maximum height 650mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"4 Intermediate Search Criteria","t":"4.4 Intermediate Vehicle search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 3 minutes.</li><li>b) Two vehicles of any type (refer to definitions) and placed in any configuration.</li><li>c) If a trailer is connected to a vehicle that will be classified as two vehicles.</li><li>d) The first vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>e) Clear access to both vehicles on all sides (minimum of 2 metres from trees, vegetation, objects, walls or boundaries), with a minimum distance of 2 metres between the two vehicles (unless attached, as in the case of a trailer).</li><li>f) Hide maximum height 650mm.</li><li>g) Hide maximum depth under vehicle 100mm.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"5 Advanced Search Criteria","t":"5 Advanced – General","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) Two hides per search element.</li><li>b) Oil of Clove Bud and/or Sweet Birch are the odours used in SSANZ Advanced trials. A combination of two different odours may be used in one hide/vessel.</li><li>c) There will be one non-food distraction in the container search, placed inside one of the cold containers.</li><li>d) No formal indication or TFR is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>e) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog.</li><li>f) Handlers are required to call the two alerts.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"5 Advanced Search Criteria","t":"5.1 Advanced Container search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time is 3 minutes.</li><li>b) May be conducted indoors or outdoors.</li><li>c) 18-20 containers set in a geometric shape.</li><li>d) The containers must be random size and made from cardboard, plastic, or metal. There can be a mixture of different types of containers.</li><li>e) Containers must remain separate — do not place containers inside other containers.</li><li>f) The containers shall be no more than 400mm in height.</li><li>g) All containers are placed at ground level.</li><li>h) The start line should be no less than one metre from the first container.</li><li>i) There is to be a minimum distance of 600mm and a maximum distance of 900mm between containers. If the containers are in rows there is to be a minimum distance of 900mm and a maximum distance of 1500 between rows.</li><li>j) Containers can be weighted to reduce accidental movement by the dog.</li><li>k) There will be one non-food distraction inside one \"cold\" container.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"5 Advanced Search Criteria","t":"5.2 Advanced Interior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 2 and 6 minutes.</li><li>b) The interior search will be two or three rooms adjacent to or adjoining each other.</li><li>c) Doors between rooms being searched must remain open.</li><li>d) The search area in total will be between 120 and 400 sqm.</li><li>e) Hide maximum height 900mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"5 Advanced Search Criteria","t":"5.3 Advanced Exterior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3 and 5 minutes.</li><li>b) Search area will be between 150 and 400sqm.</li><li>c) Hide maximum height 900mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"5 Advanced Search Criteria","t":"5.4 Advanced Vehicle search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3 and 4 minutes.</li><li>b) Three to four vehicles of any sort (refer to definition) and in any configuration.</li><li>c) If a trailer is connected to a vehicle that will be classified as two vehicles.</li><li>d) The first vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>e) Clear access to all vehicles on all sides (minimum of 2 metres from trees, vegetation, objects, walls or boundaries), with a minimum distance of 2 metres between the vehicles (unless attached, as in the case of a trailer).</li><li>f) Hides must be on different vehicles.</li><li>g) Hide maximum height 650mm.</li><li>h) Hide maximum depth under vehicle 100mm.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"6 Excellent Search Criteria","t":"6 Excellent – General","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) There will be 12 hides in total over the five searches.</li><li>b) Prior to the trial numbers will be drawn to determine how many hides will be placed in each search area, divided up as 3, 3, 3, 2, 1.</li><li>c) Judges will advise handlers of the number of hides in each search area.</li><li>d) Oil of Clove Bud, Sweet Birch, and/or Cypress (Cupressus sempervirens) are the odours used in SSANZ Excellent trials. A combination of two different odours may be used in one vessel.</li><li>e) There will be three distractions in the container search. See section Container Search General.</li><li>f) No formal indication or TFR is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>g) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog.</li><li>h) Handlers are required to call the relevant number of alerts.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"6 Excellent Search Criteria","t":"6.1 Excellent Container search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the judge and will be between 3-4 minutes.</li><li>b) May be conducted indoors or outdoors.</li><li>c) 18-22 containers set in a geometric shape.</li><li>d) The containers must be random size and made from cardboard, plastic, or metal. There must be a mixture of different types and materials of containers.</li><li>e) Containers must remain separate — do not place containers inside other containers.</li><li>f) The containers shall be no more than 400mm in height.</li><li>g) All containers are placed at ground level.</li><li>h) The start line should be no less than one metre from the first container.</li><li>i) There is to be a minimum distance of 600mm and a maximum distance of 900mm between containers. If the containers are in rows there is to be a minimum distance of 900mm and a maximum distance of 1500 between rows.</li><li>j) Containers can be weighted to reduce accidental movement by the dog.</li><li>k) There will be three distractions inside three \"cold\" containers, two non-food distractions and one food distraction.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"6 Excellent Search Criteria","t":"6.2 Excellent Interior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3-6 minutes.</li><li>b) The interior search will be between two and four rooms adjacent to or adjoining each other.</li><li>c) Doors between rooms being searched must remain open.</li><li>d) The search area in total will be between 120 and 400 sqm.</li><li>e) Hide maximum height 1100mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"6 Excellent Search Criteria","t":"6.3 Excellent Exterior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3-6 minutes.</li><li>b) Search area will be between 150 and 400sqm.</li><li>c) Hide maximum height 1100mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"6 Excellent Search Criteria","t":"6.4 Excellent Vehicle search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3-4 minutes.</li><li>b) Three to four vehicles of any sort (refer to definition) and in any configuration.</li><li>c) If a trailer is connected to a vehicle that will be classified as two vehicles.</li><li>d) The first vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>e) Clear access to all vehicles on all sides (minimum of 2 metres from trees, vegetation, objects, walls or boundaries), with a minimum distance of 2 metres between the vehicles (unless attached, as in the case of a trailer).</li><li>f) Hides must be on different vehicles.</li><li>g) Hide maximum height 1000mm, and 650mm for private passenger vehicles.</li><li>h) Hide maximum depth under vehicle 150mm, and 100mm under private passenger vehicles.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"7 Premium Search Criteria","t":"7 Premium – General","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) There will be 15 hides in total over the six searches.</li><li>b) Prior to the trial, numbers will be drawn to determine how many hides will be placed in each search area, divided up as 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 1.</li><li>c) Judges will NOT advise handlers of the number of hides in each search area.</li><li>d) Oil of Clove Bud, Sweet Birch and/or Cypress (Cupressus sempervirens) are the odours used in SSANZ Premium trials. A combination of two different odours may be used in one vessel.</li><li>e) Some hides may be inaccessible.</li><li>f) There will be four distractions in the container search. Two non-food distractions and two food distractions, placed inside four of the cold containers.</li><li>g) No formal indication or TFR is required for the handler to call an alert.</li><li>h) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog.</li><li>i) Handlers are required to call alerts, and \"finish\" when they determine all hides have been located (either before or after the Judge confirms that the find is correct).</li><li>j) If \"Finish\" is not called, the maximum time for the search will be recorded and a major fault will be incurred.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"7 Premium Search Criteria","t":"7.1 Premium Container search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the judge and will be between 3-4 minutes.</li><li>b) May be conducted indoors or outdoors.</li><li>c) 18-22 containers set in a geometric shape.</li><li>d) The containers must be random size and made from cardboard, plastic, material, or metal. There must be a mixture of different types and materials of containers.</li><li>e) Containers must remain separate — do not place containers inside other containers.</li><li>f) The containers shall be no more than 400mm in height.</li><li>g) All containers are placed at ground level.</li><li>h) The start line should be no less than one metre from the first container.</li><li>i) There is to be a minimum distance of 600mm and a maximum distance of 900mm between containers. If the containers are in rows there is to be a minimum distance of 900mm and a maximum distance of 1500 between rows.</li><li>j) Containers can be weighted to reduce accidental movement by the dog.</li><li>k) There will be four distractions inside four \"cold\" containers, two non-food distractions and two food distractions.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"7 Premium Search Criteria","t":"7.2 Premium Interior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3-6 minutes.</li><li>b) The interior search will be between two and five rooms adjacent to or adjoining each other.</li><li>c) Doors between rooms being searched must remain open.</li><li>d) The search area will total between 120 and 400 sqm.</li><li>e) Hide maximum height 1200mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"7 Premium Search Criteria","t":"7.3 Premium Exterior search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3-6 minutes.</li><li>b) Search area will be between 150 and 400sqm.</li><li>c) Hide maximum height 1200mm from the ground.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"7 Premium Search Criteria","t":"7.4 Premium Vehicle search","h":"<ul><li>a) Search time will be set by the Judge and will be between 3-4 minutes.</li><li>b) Three to five vehicles of any sort (refer to definition) and in any configuration.</li><li>c) If a trailer is connected to a vehicle that will be classified as two vehicles.</li><li>d) The first vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>e) Clear access to all vehicles on all sides (minimum of 2 metres from trees, vegetation, objects, walls or boundaries), with a minimum distance of 2 metres between the vehicles (unless attached, as in the case of a trailer).</li><li>f) Two hides may be on the same vehicle.</li><li>g) Hide maximum height 1000mm, and 650mm for private passenger vehicles.</li><li>h) Hide maximum depth under vehicle 150mm, and 100mm under private passenger vehicles.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"8 Odour Preparation","t":"8 Odour Preparation","h":"<ul><li>8.1 Three to five drops of a single essential oil are to be placed on a cotton make up remover pad at the base of a sealable glass jar. Q-tips/cotton buds are then added to the jar. Note that Q-tips/cotton buds must not have plastic handles. The jar is then sealed with an airtight lid to allow the odour to infuse. Q-Tips must be prepared a minimum of 24 and a maximum of 72 hours before use at a trial.</li><li>8.2 Two Q-tips must be placed inside the hide vessel.</li><li>8.3 At Advanced level and above, combination hides may be used by placing two different odour infused Q tips in the same vessel.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"9 Search Rules – General","t":"9.1–9.6 Handling, start line, time","h":"<ul><li>9.1 If, in the opinion of the Judge, harsh or intimidating handling has taken place they may disqualify the team from the trial.</li><li>9.2 The location of the hide/s must not be known to the handler.</li><li>9.3 Search time starts when either the dog or the handler crosses the start line. If neither the dog nor the handler crosses the start line the team will incur a minor fault. Time begins when any part of the team enters the search area.</li><li>9.4 At any time during the search the handler may use verbal and/or physical interaction, food, or a toy to encourage and support their dogs while searching.</li><li>9.5 Handlers may not lift or carry dogs during the search.</li><li>9.6 If, in the opinion of the Judge, an action is taken for safety reasons no penalties will be applied.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"9 Search Rules – General","t":"9.7 Calling alert","h":"<p>Handlers need to indicate verbally and/or visually by calling \"alert\" and/or while raising a hand above their head, to communicate to the judge that the handler believes the dog has found the hide, and the Judge will respond accordingly:</p><ul><li>a) At Novice level if the alert call is not correct the Judge will say \"False – Continue\" on the first incorrect call, and a fault will be given (see section 10.2).</li><li>b) In Intermediate level no false alerts are permitted.</li><li>c) In Advanced, Excellent, and Premium if an alert is called on a hide already found the Judge will say \"Already found – Continue\" and a fault will be given (see Section 10.2). If the handler calls the same hide a third time the search will end.</li><li>d) A Judge may ask \"Where?\" for further clarification after the alert is called. If the handler is unable to adequately indicate the location of the hide to the Judge's satisfaction, then it is considered a false alert.</li><li>e) In Novice, Intermediate, Advanced, and Excellent levels, timing concludes when the Judge has identified the final call as \"Correct\" or \"Yes\".</li><li>f) At Premium level, the timing concludes when the handler calls \"finish\".</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"9 Search Rules – General","t":"9.8 Reinforcers (food or toys)","h":"<p>Reinforcers (food or toys) may be visible.</p><ul><li>a) Handlers may take their reinforcers into the search area or leave them on an elevated location outside of the search area. When left outside the search area, the location will be specified by the judge and be the same for all participants.</li><li>b) Food must be contained either in a pocket/treat pouch if carried, or in a closed container outside the search area at the location specified.</li><li>c) Reinforcers may be used at any point during the search.</li><li>d) Handlers must not contaminate the search area with their reinforcers. No food or toys may be thrown or dropped within or adjacent to the search area.</li><li>e) If, in the Judge's opinion, the search area or surrounding has been contaminated, a major fault will be deducted.</li><li>f) Handlers must not damage the search area or anything within the search area when reinforcing their dog.</li><li>g) If the reinforcers/rewards are left outside the search area, the dog must leave the search area under control and may not be sent ahead of the handler.</li><li>h) If, in the Judge's opinion, the dog has not left the search area in a controlled manner, a major fault may be applied.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"9 Search Rules – General","t":"9.9 Off lead","h":"<p>Searches may be conducted off lead with permission from the Judge. Organisations will advise judges if the venue is not suitable for off lead searches.</p><ul><li>a) If an off-leash option is available, the handler may choose to change between off leash and on leash at any time during the search with no penalty.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"10 Faults","t":"10.1 Per Element","h":"<p>Points are awarded or deducted according to the following categories:</p><ul><li>a) If no hides are found the team will not be awarded any points for that element.</li><li>b) If the time for the search element has run out, the team will only gain points for the hide/s found.</li><li>c) If Finish is called prior to finding all the hides, the team will only gain points for the hide/s found.</li><li>d) If the handler requests help from the judge or aborts the search, the team will only gain points for the hide/s found.</li><li>e) Fouling: If the dog vomits, urinates, or defecates at any time while searching the search will end and the team will lose all points for that search.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"10 Faults","t":"10.2 Incorrect Alert Call","h":"<ul><li>a) At Novice level the first incorrect Alert Call will incur a deduction of 6 points. The Judge will call \"False – continue\" and the team may continue the search.</li><li>b) At Intermediate level an incorrect Alert Call will incur a deduction of 25 points and will end the search.</li><li>c) At Advanced, Excellent, and Premium level an incorrect Alert call will end the search but if a hide/s is found before the incorrect call, the team will still gain the points for hide/s found prior to the incorrect call.</li><li>d) At Advanced, Excellent, and Premium level the handler calling a second alert on an already found hide will incur a deduction of 6 points. The Judge will call \"Already found – continue\" and the team may continue the search. If the hide is called for the third time the search will end and incur an additional deduction of 6 points.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"10 Faults","t":"10.3 Major faults (5 points each occasion)","h":"<p>Major faults incur a deduction of 5 points on each occasion:</p><ul><li>a) Damage to the search area: If, in the judge's opinion, damage has been caused to the search area by the team (for example, excessive pawing, digging, trampling etc.) sufficient to require replacement or repair of equipment.</li><li>b) Compromising the search area: Actions of the handler or dog that have compromised the search area by causing damage or contamination that may impact on following searches/teams.</li><li>c) The judge observes the handler dropping food or rewards at any time during the search.</li><li>d) The dog damages or interferes with the hide placement.</li><li>e) Leaving the search area. The team (handler and dog) leave the search area together during a search.</li><li>f) Fouling in the vicinity of the search area including, but not limited to, marking on nearby trees or defecating.</li><li>g) Scattering or throwing of food or toys in the immediate vicinity of the search area.</li><li>h) Safety issues or other infractions that may place the dog, handler, judge, or stewards in a potentially dangerous situation.</li><li>i) Failing to call finish in Premium level.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"10 Faults","t":"10.4 Minor faults (2 points)","h":"<p>Minor Faults incur a deduction of 2 points. Faults will be given for:</p><ul><li>(a) Tangling the line.</li><li>(b) Dropping the line.</li><li>(c) Handler error.</li><li>(d) Neither the handler nor the dog crosses the start line.</li></ul><p>The judge's decision is final. No challenge or argument directly with the judge will be tolerated.</p><p>(NOTE: provisions for Elite and Ultimate levels are currently being finalised and will be added to these regulations in due course.)</p>"},{"d":"r","g":"11 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"11.1–11.13 General entry requirements","h":"<p>All entry criteria must be met for a team to participate in a SSANZ trial.</p><ul><li>11.1 All handlers must be financial members of SSANZ, and dogs registered with SSANZ. The handler must stay the same for all four elements of the trial.</li><li>11.2 Handlers under the age of 16 are considered youth handlers (See Addendum 1).</li><li>11.3 To enter an Intermediate Trial a dog must have its Novice Nosework title.</li><li>11.4 To enter an Advanced Trial a dog must have its Intermediate Nosework title.</li><li>11.5 To enter an Excellent Trial a dog must have its Advanced Nosework title.</li><li>11.6 To enter Premium a dog must have its Excellent title.</li><li>11.7 Once a dog gains a qualifying certificate at a higher level, the dog can no longer trial at the lower level. Exceptions are: when a dog is entered on both days at a double trial in two levels and qualifies on the first day at the higher level — in this instance they are permitted to run at the lower level on the second day; when a dog has entered a trial and after entries have closed they qualify out of their current level, they may trial at the level for which they have already entered. However, if the Organisation is also offering the next level up the handler may request to trial at the higher level. The decision will rest with the Organisation as to whether they can accommodate the request.</li><li>11.8 Handlers may only trial one dog at each level at a trial.</li><li>11.9 Dogs may trial with an alternate handler to their owner. The alternate handler must stay the same for all four elements of the trial.</li><li>11.10 A Judge may not enter a dog in the specific trial they are judging.</li><li>11.11 A Judge at a trial may enter a dog in another trial at the same event, at the discretion of the Organisation.</li><li>11.12 Organisers and officials may participate in a trial at the discretion of the Organisation, provided it does not impede the smooth running of the trial.</li><li>11.13 Handlers must not be involved in the setting of the hide.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"11 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"11.14 Requirements of the handler","h":"<ul><li>a) Have not been banned or excluded from trialling with SSANZ.</li><li>b) Enclosed, secure footwear must be worn at all times. A handler who presents with insecure footwear will be asked to rectify the problem. If no attempt is made to rectify, the handler may be asked to leave the trial.</li><li>c) Training vests, bags, hats etc., may be worn during searches.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"11 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"11.15 Requirements of the dog","h":"<ul><li>a) All dogs must be registered with SSANZ.</li><li>b) Over the age of six months.</li><li>c) Dogs must be in good health, fit to trial, and free from infectious diseases or parasites.</li><li>d) Dogs who raise health concerns for the judge may be excluded if a veterinary certificate is not available.</li><li>e) A Veterinary Certificate giving a brief outline and stating a dog is fit to trial is required if an injury/disability/illness is obvious. A vet release form is available from SSANZ on the scentsports.nz website for completion prior to the trial.</li><li>f) Dogs must have a current vaccination record, issued by a registered professional.</li><li>g) Dogs attending the trial should be clean. Dogs who are excessively dirty will be excluded from trialling that day.</li><li>h) Dogs must have a current registration with their local council.</li><li>i) Bitches in season are NOT allowed to participate, nor permitted at the venue where the trial is held.</li><li>j) Notification is required to be given to the organisation at the earliest indication that a dog needs to be scratched for any reason.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"11 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"11.16 Menacing dogs","h":"<ul><li>a) A dog that has been classified as menacing or dangerous under the Dog Control Act (1996), may participate in a SSANZ Trial at the discretion of the Organisation.</li><li>b) Additional information must be provided to the Organiser on request. This can include, but is not limited to: (i) Owner name. (ii) Owner address. (iii) Dog name. (iv) Dog breed. (v) Dog colour. (vi) Type of classification.</li><li>c) A copy of the exemption document must be provided with the entry form.</li><li>d) A dog that has been classified as menacing under the Dog Control Act (1996), and which has been granted an exemption under Section 33E (5) of the Dog Control Act (1996) by the local territorial authority that has jurisdiction over the trial location, may participate at a SSANZ Trial within the parameters of the exemption.</li><li>e) SSANZ Organisations may still require dogs with a menacing classification to be muzzled at all times, even if they have an exemption.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"12 Equipment","t":"12 Equipment","h":"<ul><li>a) Lead length and type is at the handler's discretion.</li><li>b) Leads may be attached to a suitable harness or collar for the searches. Correctly fitted non-restrictive harnesses are permitted for use in SSANZ trials.</li><li>c) If the line is to be attached to the harness it must be affixed to the top (spine), not to any front clip that may be present.</li><li>d) Check, choke, slip, prong, citronella collars, head halters, and Electronic collars are not permitted in the search areas.</li><li>e) Optional equipment worn by the dog such as coats, boots, or muzzles other than those outlined in 10.d, may be permitted at the discretion of the Judge, and permission will not be unreasonably withheld.</li><li>f) Belly bands or incontinence nappies are permitted. If a dog lifts its hind leg or squats as if to pee while searching it will still be faulted as a fouling incident.</li><li>g) All equipment must be correctly fitted.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"13 Trial Protocol","t":"13.1–13.8 Trial protocol","h":"<ul><li>13.1 No unofficial odour may be brought anywhere onsite, other than in the confines of a private vehicle.</li><li>13.2 A briefing will be given before the trial starts. a) Out of bounds areas will be outlined. b) Health and safety details for the venue will be advised. c) General housekeeping and details of the trial will be covered. d) There will be an opportunity for triallists to ask questions.</li><li>13.3 If a handler has any concerns regarding the running of the trial or regarding any set up of a search area/judging matter, they must in the first instance bring up their concern with the Organiser. If the issue can not be resolved by the Organisation, members can complete a Trial Feedback Form which can be found on the SSANZ website and Facebook page.</li><li>13.4 Safety concerns must be raised immediately with the Organiser. The Organiser may pause the trial while the safety concerns are investigated and mitigated where necessary. All attendees should be notified of safety concerns if they cannot be mitigated. If members do not believe safety concerns are dealt with adequately, a Trial Feedback Form should be completed after the event.</li><li>13.5 Search areas will be defined visually and may also be broadly outlined during the briefing. a) Handlers are NOT allowed in the search areas until it is their turn to trial unless authorised by the Judge or delegated official. b) If a trialist enters the search area without authorisation they may be disqualified from the trial.</li><li>13.6 Handlers are prohibited from discussing any detail in relation to the search or the operation of the search with any other participant or spectator until ALL teams have concluded that search.</li><li>13.7 If, in the opinion of the Judge, harsh or intimidating handling has taken place they may deduct all points for that search, and may disqualify the handler from the trial.</li><li>13.8 The Judge or Organiser may ask a trialist to leave if it is deemed necessary in the interests of the safety and welfare of other dogs, handlers, stewards, and/or judges, or for bad sportsmanship, including but not limited to disrespectful treatment of volunteers, officials, or fellow trialists, and intentional disclosure/knowledge of the hide locations.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"13 Trial Protocol","t":"13.9 Recording of the event","h":"<p>Recording of the event is allowable under the following circumstances:</p><p><strong>Recording of event - Handler</strong></p><ul><li>a) A handler may wear and use visible head or chest mounted video equipment.</li><li>b) Recordings of any search must not be shared with anyone until the trial has finished.</li><li>c) Recordings cannot be used to challenge the judge's decision.</li><li>d) Use of any recording equipment must not delay the start of any search, and the judge and organisation reserve the right to refuse set up of recording equipment.</li><li>e) A handler may ask for permission to have a separate person record their search and the following will apply: i) Permission will be granted at the discretion of the Organisation and/or Judge. ii) Any person recording a search must follow all directions given by the Judge or other designated official. iii) The Judge or designated official reserves the right to ask a person recording searches to leave at any time.</li></ul><p><strong>Recording of event - Organisation</strong></p><ul><li>a) An organisation may provide a photographer / videographer for some searches.</li><li>b) If any recording is to occur at a trial, this must be advised by the Organisation at the time of trial entry or at the briefing.</li><li>c) Individuals must be given the choice to agree or refuse any photos or video being taken of them, their dog, and/or their searches.</li><li>d) With permission from the handler, photos or video taken may be used by the Organisation for purposes of sharing on social media, future advertising, and/or with sponsors, and must be made available to the handler in question on request.</li><li>e) If a professional photographer is at the venue, photos and/or video may be available to purchase, and may only be shared with the permission of the subject of the photo or video.</li><li>f) Organisations are not responsible for recording each and every search.</li><li>g) Videographer(s) arranged by the Organisation may take photos or video of handlers, dogs and searches at the trial venue.</li><li>h) Organisations may choose not to allow handlers to record their own searches.</li><li>i) The handler, Judge, or designated official reserves the right to ask the videographer to leave if they believe that is appropriate.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"14 Organisation or Host Requirements","t":"14.1–14.4 Hosting and advertising","h":"<ul><li>14.1 To host a SSANZ trial the Organisation must be a current financial SSANZ Organisational Member.</li><li>14.2 Trials must be run under the SSANZ Rules and Regulations.</li><li>14.3 Organisers must apply to have a trial recognised as a SSANZ trial by: a) Emailing an application with details to the SSANZ secretary. b) Emailing the application at least three months prior to the trial date. c) The application must include the intended date of the trial/s, venue, judge/s and levels. d) SSANZ reserves the right to request additional information prior to giving approval.</li><li>14.4 Upon approval, trial advertising must state the following: a) \"This trial is being conducted under the rules and regulations of the Scent Sports Association of New Zealand (SSANZ).\" b) All trials must advertise a closing date, no less than 21 days prior to the trial date, and entries must be kept open until that date. c) Judges d) Venue of trial e) Any awards or prizes.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"14 Organisation or Host Requirements","t":"14.5 Ballots and wait lists","h":"<p>Only when the trial entries have closed can ballots (if required) be carried out. On the closing date for entries, team spots will be allocated in the following order:</p><ul><li>(i) Judges and stewards (see definition) of the event will be accepted first.</li><li>(ii) Dogs that have fewer than two qualifying certificates in that level will be accepted next.</li><li>(iii) Dogs that have between two and five qualifying certificates in that level will be allocated a place by random draw.</li><li>(iv) If there is a ballot, teams that do not receive a place will be added to a wait list.</li><li>(vi) The wait list will be ordered according to the ballot drawn. The wait list will be emailed to handlers who were unsuccessful after the ballot. Should a team withdraw their entry, handlers on the wait list will be offered the empty slot in order of ballot drawn.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"14 Organisation or Host Requirements","t":"14.6–14.16 Organiser responsibilities","h":"<ul><li>14.6 SSANZ will not be responsible or held liable for any accident, illness, or injury that may occur at a trial. SSANZ encourages all Organisations to hold public liability insurance for their trials.</li><li>14.7 Organisers can run searches and levels in any order to suit their requirements, provided these meet the criteria outlined for each individual search.</li><li>14.8 The Organiser is responsible for contracting SSANZ Judges. a) The Organiser and Judge are to communicate between themselves directly and decide on any monetary or other remuneration – this is not the responsibility of SSANZ.</li><li>14.9 Organisations are responsible for providing the correct equipment and search areas as required by the SSANZ Regulations, including the odour infused Q-tips and a suitable range of hides/vessels. Judges may prefer to provide their own q-tips and hides/vessels - it is the Organisations responsibility to liaise with judges in this regard. A raised table, chair, or other suitable platform must be available near the start line of each search area for handlers to place personal effects (and reinforcers if the handler chooses).</li><li>14.10 Entry fees charged to enter a trial are set by the Organiser. a) Entry fees are the property of the Organiser.</li><li>14.11 The Organiser is responsible for obtaining official SSANZ Qualifier Certificates.</li><li>14.12 At their discretion, Organisers may present additional participation certificates/awards, but these are not officially recognised by SSANZ for the purposes of any titles.</li><li>14.13 The Organiser may set a competitive aspect to a trial or provide prizes if they wish. If there is a competitive aspect prizes in a trial will be awarded based on the following criteria: a) Teams with no faults are placed above faulted teams. b) Awards and prizes are the responsibility of the Organiser.</li><li>14.14 Organisers may hold additional novelty scent events at SSANZ trials with the understanding they are conducted in a manner that is consistent with SSANZ protocols.</li><li>14.15 Organisers must forward a copy of all resulting scores and search times to the SSANZ Secretary within 7 days of the trial's conclusion.</li><li>14.16 Organisers must keep records of the trial for 12 months after the trial date including but not limited to: a) Application to hold a SSANZ trial. b) Entry forms. c) Trial results. d) Menacing dog information and exemptions. e) Financial records. f) Records of any incidents, complaints or safety issues.</li></ul><p>These regulations are based on a combined interpretation of rules from the USA and NACSW, modified following initial Novice tests conducted in February 2015 and adopted for SSANZ since July 2019.</p>"},{"d":"r","g":"Definitions","t":"Definitions","h":"<ul><li><strong>Alert:</strong> Word used by the handler to communicate to the Judge that the dog has indicated the location of the hide.</li><li><strong>Accessible hide:</strong> A hide that the dog can get close enough to place his nose on but not pick up with his/her mouth.</li><li><strong>Change of behaviour:</strong> Any behaviour by the dog which makes it clear to the Judge and/or handler that the dog has encountered odour.</li><li><strong>Cold Container:</strong> A container that has not ever been used to hold a hide.</li><li><strong>Event:</strong> Several trials run in the same time frame at the same venue.</li><li><strong>Exterior:</strong> Examples of areas include the exterior of a building, a parking lot, grassy paddock, courtyard, deck/patio, space between buildings, covered outdoor deck/patio, playground, basketball/tennis courts etc. The spirit of this search is that it is outside. Please do not cheat and pretend that a garage with the door open is suitable for use as an exterior for example.</li><li><strong>Finish:</strong> The handler is required to call \"Finish\" in Premium level and above when they believe all hides in the search area have been located and called. The \"Finish\" call stops the official search time, and the search. If \"Finish\" is not called, the maximum time will be allocated to that search. In Premium level a major fault will be applied.</li><li><strong>Handler:</strong> The person participating in the search with the dog.</li><li><strong>Hide:</strong> Vessel containing odour.</li><li><strong>Hot Container:</strong> A container that is being, or has been used for target odour at any stage.</li><li><strong>Interior room:</strong> An indoor space that is enclosed by permanent floor-to-ceiling walls, partitions, or glazing and has a defined boundary separating it from adjoining spaces. To be considered a separate room, the space must be substantially enclosed and have a clear means of entry or access. Partial walls, dividing walls that do not fully enclose a space, furniture, shelving, screens, or other internal features do not create separate rooms. Toilet cubicles, shower cubicles, changing cubicles, and similar subdivisions that do not extend from floor to ceiling are not considered separate rooms.</li><li><strong>Inaccessible hide:</strong> A hide that the dog is not able to place his nose on. A hide may be inaccessible due to reach, containment, or height.</li><li><strong>Judge:</strong> Official scorer at a trial, SSANZ approved Judges.</li><li><strong>Level:</strong> One specific level of a SSANZ trial or title e.g., Novice.</li><li><strong>Official:</strong> A person commissioned by an Organiser to help run a trial.</li><li><strong>Organiser:</strong> A current financial Organisation member of SSANZ.</li><li><strong>Owner:</strong> The registered owner of a dog.</li><li><strong>Search:</strong> One element of a level e.g., container search.</li><li><strong>Start Line:</strong> A clear line marked by cones or other items that indicate where the search will begin. The search time will start when either the dog or handler crosses this line. A minor fault will be deducted if neither the dog nor the handler crosses the line.</li><li><strong>Steward:</strong> The person who is working with a Judge to brief and/or time keep at any element.</li><li><strong>Team:</strong> A dog and handler for a level at a trial.</li><li><strong>TFR:</strong> Trained Final Response e.g. sit, down, stare, sustained nose touch.</li><li><strong>Trial:</strong> One level involving all four elements.</li><li><strong>Vehicle:</strong> At novice level the vehicle must be a car, ute, or minivan. At the higher levels vehicles and towable implements can include, but are not limited to, buses, trucks, trailers (car, truck, animal floats, caravans), tractors, four wheelers, and forklifts. Two-wheeler motorbikes, ride-on lawn mowers, wheelbarrows, broomsticks, rickshaws, horse-drawn carriages, vacuum cleaners, etc are not allowed to be used for a vehicle search. If a trailer/caravan/animal float is connected to a vehicle, that will be classified as two vehicles.</li><li><strong>Vicinity of the search area:</strong> The area surrounding the designated search area.</li><li><strong>Volunteer:</strong> A person helping in no official capacity.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"Addendum 1 – Youth Handler Policy","t":"Addendum 1 – Youth Handler Policy","h":"<p>Handlers younger than 16 years of age can trial at SSANZ trials with the following:</p><ul><li>Be a member of SSANZ.</li><li>Must have an adult guardian accompany and be responsible for the youth and the dog at the trial.</li><li>As the youth team requires the adult guardian to support them at the individual elements of the trial if the adult is also trialling in the same level the adult to be placed earlier than the youth in the running orders.</li></ul><p>The guardian is to be nominated with the entry. A statement is completed as part of the entry as who is the adult guardian responsible for the team acknowledging the below statement:</p><p><em>I acknowledge that under the Dog Control Act 1996 I am legally responsible for the dog's control and compliance. I will remain on site and within line-of-sight during all runs, carry a leash, and ensure compliance with all council bylaws and event rules.</em></p><p>If it has been noted on the entry form that the dog is reactive the guardian must accompany the team at all times.</p><p>When the guardian goes to the search areas, they:</p><ul><li>Must stand where instructed and not impact any of the search areas.</li><li>Cannot speak or move while a team is searching.</li><li>Will not coach the team between or during elements.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"Addendum 2 – Photography Guidelines","t":"Addendum 2 – Photography Guidelines","h":"<p>In principle, photographers and videographers are welcome at SSANZ trials, however there are a few things that should be taken into account for the safety and well-being of all.</p><p><strong>Recording of Event - Handler.</strong> This is covered under Regulation 13.8. The onus is on you to ensure that you adhere to these regulations and it is courteous to confirm with the judge that they are happy for you to record your search.</p><p>It may also be OK for a friend who has either completed their search or is not running a dog later in that trial, to come through with you to take photos / video on your behalf. You should always ask the presiding judge if they are happy with this. Please do not assume it is OK.</p><p>Photographers are required to remain outside the search area and should ask the judge where they would like them to stand for the duration of the search. Your request to bring someone else in may be declined if the space is crowded or not conducive to recording for any reason, for example an interior space where the start line is just through a doorway. In the event that a judge declines or asks your friend to leave, they must comply immediately without further discussion.</p><p><strong>Recording of Event - Organisation.</strong> The remainder of this document is dedicated to official photographers that have a prior agreement with the host organisation to either photograph or video parts of the trial (see regulation 13.9). Official photographers have a responsibility to the trialing teams not to interfere with the search, distract the dog or handler, or provide any clue as to the location of any hide(s).</p>"},{"d":"r","g":"Addendum 2 – Photography Guidelines","t":"Addendum 2 – Photographer best practice","h":"<ul><li>1. Ask the organisation in advance if any teams have indicated on their entry that they do not wish to be photographed / recorded. Ideally, have a copy of the running order so that you can keep track of when they are due in any search that you are covering.</li><li>2. Liaise with the judges before the trial briefing to check that they are OK for you to a) be present, and b) confirm where they would like you to locate yourself. While you may have some preference for best light and other factors, the judge's decision is final.</li><li>3. Ideally the dog and handler will be able to see you from the start line so there is no risk of them being startled.</li><li>4. Experiment with and finalise your lens and camera settings before any teams come through. A big zoom lens is generally best.</li><li>5. Disable any shutter noise so that there is no clicking to distract the teams, or even give them a clue that they are close to the hide location. Take a range of shots, not just targeting the hide location(s), especially as not all dogs will be successful and some like photos of their dog working.</li><li>6. Arrange with the organisation to attend the handler briefing with the judges to advise people which elements you will be covering and give handlers the opportunity to speak to you regarding any specific needs they or their dog have. Let them know how you will make photos available after the event.</li><li>7. Minimise movement and chatter in the search area especially when there are teams present. The judge and steward need to concentrate. Definitely don't commentate on the search or utter \"call it\" under your breath.</li><li>8. If a dog is distracted by you or your camera, slowly lower your camera, turning the lens away from them, and turn or lower your head to break any eye contact with the dog. Note, they may bark, stare, or simply want to approach and sniff. Do not attempt to interact with the dog or handler at all.</li><li>9. If a team has indicated that their dog is reactive, but they would still like to have photos taken, you need to ensure that you will be safe. Make sure the handler is comfortable with where you are before starting their search, especially if they intend to complete the search off-lead. You may need to exit the area for safety so it is useful to have already identified an exit route that does not take you past the start line.</li><li>10. If you hope to move from one area to another while a trial is still running, you also need to have thought about your route in advance so that you minimise the risk of disrupting teams.</li><li>11. Never enter or walk through the search area without the express permission of the judge.</li><li>12. Always check with the handler concerned before using any image outside of what has previously been agreed with the organisation for sharing.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"Addendum 3 – Integrated Advanced & Excellent Trials","t":"Addendum 3 – Integrated Advanced & Excellent Trials","h":"<p>SSANZ would like to support Organisations to run Excellent Trials. In order to facilitate this, Organisations will have the option to run \"integrated\" Advanced and Excellent trials until the end of 2026. This integrated format will be reviewed in January 2027.</p><p>Organisations may still opt to run either Advanced or Excellent as stand-alone trials if they wish and the option to run \"integrated\" trials applies solely to Advanced and Excellent.</p><p><strong>Integrated Trials Outline.</strong> The parameters of Excellent Searches are the same as Advanced. With a little forethought and planning as outlined below, an Organisation can integrate running an Excellent Trial with an Advanced Trial, utilising the same search areas for Containers, Interior, Vehicle and Exterior. They then only have to set up one additional search for Excellent which may be selected from any of the 4 elements.</p><ul><li>Organisations would have to advertise that they are running an \"Integrated Advanced and Excellent Trial\".</li><li>Judges would be invited for both Advanced and Excellent, and would judge the same element(s) at each level.</li><li>Handlers would only be able to run 1 dog and to choose between entering Advanced or Excellent, ie would not be able to enter one dog in Advanced and another dog in Excellent.</li><li>Results will need to be on the appropriate separate Result Sheets.</li><li>The draw by the judges for the number of hides in each Excellent search would need to be completed before the running order is set.</li></ul><p>Once the draw for the hides is done, the running orders can be prepared using the following format for those searches that will be run by both levels:</p><ul><li>1. For any Excellent searches that have drawn 3 hides, the judge will first set up the search for Advanced with 2 hides. Once all Advanced teams have run, the judge will place out the third hide, wait an additional 15 minutes, and then the Excellent teams may run through.</li><li>2. For a search that has drawn 2 hides, it will be the same for both levels. Ideally Advanced teams would run first, followed by the Excellent teams.</li><li>3. For a search that has drawn 1 hide, the judge will first set up the search for Excellent with a single hide. Once all Excellent teams have run, the judge will then place out the second hide, wait an additional 15 minutes, and then the Advanced teams may run through.</li><li>4. For the container search, the additional distractions will need to be in place for Excellent. If this search draws 1 hide and so is run for Excellent first the containers that have had additional distractions (for Excellent) in them will need to be replaced with cold containers.</li></ul><p>Trainee Advanced judges are allowed to use Excellent searches for mentoring appointments. Therefore, it would be possible for them to enter Advanced with their own dog and then accept a mentoring appointment in the 5th (additional) Excellent search, with prior agreement from the host organisation and coaching Judge.</p>"},{"d":"r","g":"Addendum 4 – Reuse of Areas","t":"Addendum 4 – Context & Guidelines","h":"<p><strong>Context.</strong> SSANZ relies on our organisations to provide search areas that are safe, meet the regulatory requirements for the various elements, and allow for a fair trial. This means search areas must be free from excessive or unnecessary distractions. Molecules of essential oil that have drifted, adhered to, been absorbed by surfaces, or are lingering in an area from an earlier trial can potentially cause dogs to have to investigate and eliminate them before sourcing the intended hide/s, all within their restricted search time. Additionally, a handler may have to read the situation and interrupt their dog's search to redirect them. When areas are reused, there is a risk of previous fouling or other contamination causing further distraction. Furthermore, dogs trialing at both levels may be disadvantaged if they rely on memory.</p><p><strong>Guidelines.</strong> Spaces selected for trials should not have been used for any essential-oil based nosework activity in the preceding three months.</p><p>Ideally, all search areas used at an event will be clean, unique and completely separate from one another.</p><p>The guidelines below are designed to uphold best practice in circumstances where any portion of a search area must be reused, OR search area boundaries are shared and there is a risk of odour drift between areas. (These are collectively referred to as \"affected areas\" below.)</p>"},{"d":"r","g":"Addendum 4 – Reuse of Areas","t":"Addendum 4 – Organisational Responsibilities","h":"<ul><li>1. Avoid Same-Day Reuse: Aim not to reuse areas on the same day, unless for: exterior searches where earlier hides have been placed on removable objects, including but not limited to vehicles and containers, OR neighbouring but not overlapping search areas where every effort is taken to air out the space, and earlier hides have been placed on removable objects, including but not limited to vehicles and containers, OR overlapping search areas, where every effort is taken to air out the space, and earlier hides have been placed on removable objects, ideally at a reasonable distance from the other search area boundary.</li><li>2. Provide Adequate Equipment: Supply sufficient vehicles and containers to ensure that no \"hot\" vehicle or container is reused during the event.</li><li>3. Notify Judges: Inform judges in advance of all affected search areas.</li><li>4. Hold Daily Pre-Trial Meetings: Conduct mandatory pre-trial meetings each morning with judges to review the day's search areas, identify affected areas, and discuss the operational plan. Care may need to be exercised if a judge will be trialing at one of the affected levels. If judges will not be in attendance until later in the day, ensure that adjoining or overlapping boundaries of any affected areas from the morning are clearly marked (masking tape or similar) to avoid any misunderstanding.</li><li>5. Air Out Search Spaces: Make every effort to \"air out\" affected areas between searches. Time allocated for ventilation must account for the space size, airflow, the proximity and the duration that previous hide/s were out.</li><li>6. Restrict Staging Areas: Ensure that staging of teams does not occur within the boundaries of a later search area.</li></ul>"},{"d":"r","g":"Addendum 4 – Reuse of Areas","t":"Addendum 4 – Judges' Responsibilities","h":"<ul><li>1. Collaborate with other Judges: Coordinate with fellow judges who are allocated linked areas to confirm boundaries, discuss sections of the search space, and plan hide placements to minimise impact. If another judge is entered, commit to debrief them fully after your search has completed.</li><li>2. Select Hide Placements with Care: Determine where and how the earlier hides can be placed either on removable objects or at a reasonable distance from the later search boundary. Note: Placing hides on removable objects is mandatory if a hide falls within a search area that will be reused later in the same event. SSANZ does not permit marking previous hide placements within a search area.</li><li>3. Promptly Remove Hides: Remove hides immediately after the last dog completes the search.</li><li>4. Relocate Removable Objects: Where a hide has been placed on a removable item, remove it from the area as soon as practical to a pre-determined location well clear of subsequent searches (e.g., the results room). Advise the organisers so items are returned before leaving the venue.</li><li>5. Ventilate Indoor Areas: For indoor search areas, leave doors and windows open whenever practical to encourage odour dispersal.</li><li>6. Maintain Strict Odour Hygiene: Always practice proper odour hygiene when setting hides: a) Ensure the Q-tips are fully contained within the scent vessel and never make contact with external surfaces. b) Ensure gloves used to handle raw scent materials/Q-tips are never used to touch the exterior of the vessel or to place the hide in position. c) Think carefully before placing any odour-containing jar, carry case, or other receptacle onto a surface either in or immediately adjacent to the search area where it might leave traces of odour and create a distraction.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"Definition","t":"Buried Hides – Definition of Nosework","h":"<p>Nosework was created for the purpose of allowing dogs to use their natural olfactory abilities to work in partnership with their handler and to locate specific target odours.</p><p>Nosework is an uninterrupted performance by a dog and handler to demonstrate working as a team and can easily be performed in a wide variety of locations.</p><p>Each participating team will be evaluated on their ability to search for and locate target odour/s.</p><p>The arrangement of the searches in Nosework trials are modelled on those of traditional working detection dogs. The overall performance should convey an image of fun, enthusiasm, and highlight how naturally and effectively the team works together.</p><p>Our aim is to provide a safe and enriching environment that promotes a trusting and lasting relationship. Harsh handling will not be tolerated and may result in immediate disqualification and the handler not permitted to participate in any further searches on that day.</p><p>The primary focus of the trial is to achieve a qualifying score. Performance/competitive targets may be included at the discretion of the organisers when providing prizes and awards.</p><p><strong>Buried Hide Trials are separate and distinct from Nosework Trials.</strong></p>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.1 Novice Buried Hides Title (BHNov)","h":"<p>Objective for Novice Buried Hide is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly identify a single target odour.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Novice Buried Hide Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a current financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) A dog must attain eight SSANZ Novice Buried Hide qualifying certificates, consisting of a minimum of four in the Water searches, and four in the Sand searches under a minimum of two different judges at two different locations. See section 14, Definitions. Further criteria is outlined in 1.2.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.2 Novice Buried Hide Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) The team must successfully complete a Buried Hide search in the allocated time.</li><li>b) A minimum score of 21 points out of a maximum of 25 is required.</li><li>c) The team must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ Buried Hide rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.3 Intermediate Buried Hides Title (BHInt)","h":"<p>Objective for Intermediate Buried Hide is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly identify two target odours in two different containers.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Intermediate title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a current financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) A dog must attain eight SSANZ Intermediate Buried Hide qualifying trial certificates, consisting of four in the water searches and four in the sand searches under a minimum of two different judges at two different locations. See section 14, Definitions. Further criteria is outlined in 1.4.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.4 Intermediate Buried Hide Qualifying Certificate","h":"<p>To attain an Intermediate Buried Hide qualifying certificate the team must successfully complete a Buried Hide search in the allocated time.</p><ul><li>a) An Intermediate Buried Hide search is worth 50 points. Each hide has equal value.</li><li>b) A minimum score of 46 is required.</li><li>c) The team must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.5 Advanced Buried Hides Title (BHAdv)","h":"<p>Objective for Advanced Buried Hides is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly identify three target odours in one search that consists of a combination of an equal number of Water containers and Sand containers.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Advanced Buried Hide Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a current financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) A dog must attain six SSANZ Advanced Buried Hides qualifying trial certificates under a minimum of two different judges at two different locations. See section 14, Definitions. Further criteria is outlined in 1.6.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.6 Advanced Buried Hide Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) The team must successfully complete a Buried Hide search and locate all odours within the allocated time.</li><li>b) An Advanced Buried Hide is worth 75 points. Each hide has equal value.</li><li>c) A minimum score of 71 is required.</li><li>d) The team must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.7 Excellent Buried Hides Title (BHExc)","h":"<p>Objective for Excellent Buried Hides is to demonstrate the ability of the team to correctly identify all target odours present. There will be between zero and five hides, the total number unknown to the handler on the day.</p><p>Requirements to be granted the Excellent Buried Hide Title:</p><ul><li>a) Handler must be a current financial member of SSANZ.</li><li>b) A dog must attain six SSANZ Excellent Buried Hides qualifying trial certificates under a minimum of two different judges at two different locations. See section 14, Definitions. Further criteria is outlined in 1.8.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"1 Level Requirements – Buried Hide Awards","t":"1.8 Excellent Buried Hide Qualifying Certificate","h":"<ul><li>a) The team must complete a buried hide search and locate all odours within the allocated time.</li><li>b) An Excellent Buried Search is worth 100 points.</li><li>c) A minimum score of 96 is required.</li><li>d) The dog must meet any other relevant criteria outlined in the SSANZ rules.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"2 Setup of Buried Searches – General","t":"2.1–2.15 General setup and containers","h":"<ul><li>2.1 The hot containers will be placed by the judge or designated official a minimum of 15 minutes before the first team commences searching.</li><li>2.2 The hide must not be identifiable to the team in the normal course of the search.</li><li>2.3 The search area should be clean and safe.</li><li>2.4 The search area will be clearly defined by boundary markers, structures, or walls.</li><li>2.5 The containers must be placed a minimum of one and half metres from any boundary.</li><li>2.6 There must be a clearly defined start line no less than three metres from the nearest container/s.</li><li>2.7 All containers used will be clean with no contamination from foodstuffs or strong smelling substances.</li><li>2.8 For each substrate, containers must be identical – uniform in size and type – and be made of plastic.</li><li>2.9 Containers may be: Ten-litre round containers no deeper than 260mm; Square containers with a minimum width of 250mm and a maximum width of 300mm, with a minimum depth of 160mm and a maximum depth of 300mm; Rectangle containers with a minimum length of 300mm and a maximum length of 500mm, a minimum width of 200mm and a maximum width of 350mm, with a minimum depth of 160mm and a maximum depth of 300mm.</li><li>2.10 Containers must be completely covered with a metal grid to prevent the dogs from making contact with the substrate. The grid must be secured to the container.</li><li>2.11 All containers must be placed at ground level.</li><li>2.12 Spacing: a minimum of one and half metres and a maximum of three meters between one edge of a container and the edge of another container.</li><li>2.13 Searches may be conducted indoors or outdoors.</li><li>2.14 Additional hot containers and blank containers will be on standby in case any have to be replaced.</li><li>2.15 Also refer to Set up of Search Area – level specific and substrate specific.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"2 Setup of Buried Searches – General","t":"Substrate specific – Sand","h":"<ul><li>a) Only use purchased river sand which must be dry.</li><li>b) 2 x scented Q-tips will be placed inside the scent vessel.</li><li>c) The scent vessel must be a round stainless steel mesh tea ball/tea infuser with a maximum diameter of 50mm.</li><li>d) The scent vessel must be placed at the bottom of the container.</li><li>e) There must be 25mm of sand covering the scent vessel.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"2 Setup of Buried Searches – General","t":"Substrate specific – Water","h":"<ul><li>a) Water must be potable (drinking quality) water.</li><li>b) 2 x scented Q-tips will be placed loose in the \"hot\" container(s).</li><li>c) Each \"cold\" (blank) water container will have 2 unscented Q-tips placed loose so all containers look the same.</li><li>d) Q-tips must have paper shafts, not bamboo.</li><li>e) All water containers must be filled with 100mm of water.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"3 Novice Buried Search Criteria","t":"3 Novice Buried Water search","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) One hide per search.</li><li>b) Oil of Clove Bud (Syzygium aromaticum syn. Eugenia caryophyllata) will be the odour used in SSANZ Buried Hide Novice trials.</li><li>c) Eight containers placed in two parallel rows of four, with no offset.</li><li>d) Containers must be identical – uniform in size and type – and be made of plastic.</li><li>e) No formal indication or trained final response is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>f) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog.</li><li>g) Search time is two minutes.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"3 Novice Buried Search Criteria","t":"3 Novice Buried Sand search","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) One hide per search.</li><li>b) Oil of Clove Bud (Syzygium aromaticum syn. Eugenia caryophyllata) will be the odour used in SSANZ Buried Hide Novice trials.</li><li>c) Eight containers placed in two parallel rows of four, with no offset.</li><li>d) Containers must be identical – uniform in size and type – and be made of plastic.</li><li>e) No formal indication or trained final response is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>f) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog.</li><li>g) Search time is two minutes.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"4 Intermediate Buried Search Criteria","t":"4 Intermediate Buried Water search","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) Two hides in two separate containers per search, one of each odour.</li><li>b) Oil of Clove Bud (Syzygium aromaticum syn. Eugenia caryophyllata) and oil of Sweet Birch (Betula lenta), will be the odours used in SSANZ Buried Hide Intermediate trials.</li><li>c) Twelve containers placed in any pattern.</li><li>d) Containers must be identical – uniform in size and type – and be made of plastic.</li><li>e) No formal indication or trained final response is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>f) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog at each hide called.</li><li>g) Search time is three minutes.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"4 Intermediate Buried Search Criteria","t":"4.1 Intermediate Buried Sand search","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) Two hides in two separate containers per search, one of each odour.</li><li>b) Oil of Clove Bud (Syzygium aromaticum syn. Eugenia caryophyllata) and oil of Sweet Birch (Betula lenta), will be the odours used in SSANZ Buried Hide Intermediate trials.</li><li>c) Twelve containers placed in any pattern.</li><li>d) Containers must be identical – uniform in size and type – and be made of plastic.</li><li>e) No formal indication or trained final response is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>f) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog at each hide called.</li><li>g) Search time is three minutes.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"5 Advanced Buried Search Criteria","t":"5 Advanced Buried search (sand and water)","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) Three hides in three separate containers per search element, one of each odour.</li><li>b) Oil of Clove Bud (Syzygium aromaticum syn. Eugenia caryophyllata), oil of Sweet Birch (Betula lenta), and oil of Cypress (Cupressus sempervirens) will be the odours used in SSANZ Advanced trials.</li><li>c) Fourteen containers placed in any pattern. Seven identical containers must be filled with sand, and seven identical containers must be filled with water. The sand and water containers may, but are not required to, be identical.</li><li>d) No formal indication or trained final response is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>e) The judge should observe a change of behaviour from the dog at each hide called.</li><li>f) Search time is four minutes.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"6 Excellent Buried Search Criteria","t":"6 Excellent Buried search (sand and water)","h":"<p>In addition to the general criteria:</p><ul><li>a) Number of hides from 0 to five, unknown to the handler.</li><li>b) The odour used in each hide is at the discretion of the judge.</li><li>c) Oil of Clove Bud (Syzygium aromaticum syn. Eugenia caryophyllata), oil of Sweet Birch (Betula lenta), and oil of Cypress (Cupressus sempervirens) will be the odours used in SSANZ Buried Hide Excellent trials.</li><li>d) Eighteen containers placed in any pattern. Nine identical containers must be filled with sand, and nine identical containers must be filled with water. The sand and water containers may, but are not required to be identical.</li><li>e) No formal indication or trained final response is required for the handler to call alert.</li><li>f) A change of behaviour should be observed by the judge at each hide called.</li><li>g) Handlers are required to call alerts, and \"finish\" once they determine all hides have been found (either before or after the Judge confirms that the find is correct).</li><li>h) If \"finish\" is not called, a major fault will be incurred and the team will time out.</li><li>i) Search time limit is four minutes.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"7 Odour Preparation for Buried Hides","t":"7 Odour Preparation for Buried Hides","h":"<ul><li>7.1 Specific for Buried Hides: Q-tips (must have paper shafts) are to be prepared with one drop of essential oil applied directly to the Q-tip no less than 24 and no more than 72 hours before use and then stored in a glass jar with an airtight lid before being used.</li><li>7.2 Two Q-tips are to be used in each container.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"8 Search Rules – General","t":"8.1–8.6 On leash, start line, time","h":"<ul><li>8.1 All Buried Hide Searches are to be completed on leash.</li><li>8.2 The location of the hide/s must not be known to the handler.</li><li>8.3 The team is expected to start the search by crossing the start line. If neither the dog nor the handler crosses the start line, the team will incur a minor fault.</li><li>8.4 Time begins when any part of the team enters the search area.</li><li>8.5 Once over the start line the team can search the containers in any pattern and from any angle.</li><li>8.6 At any time during the search the handler may use verbal and/or physical interaction, use food or a toy, to encourage and support their dogs while searching.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"8 Search Rules – General","t":"8.7 Calling alert","h":"<p>Handlers need to indicate verbally and/or visually by calling \"alert\" and/or raising a hand above their head, to communicate to the judge that the handler believes the dog has found the hide, and the judge will respond accordingly.</p><ul><li>a) In Intermediate and above, if the alert called is on a hide already found the Judge will say \"Already found – Continue\" and a fault will be given (see Section 9). If the handler calls an already-found hide a third time the search will finish.</li><li>b) A judge may ask \"Where?\" for further clarification after the alert is called. If the handler is unable to indicate the correct container, then it is considered a false alert.</li><li>c) Timing concludes when the judge has identified the call as \"correct\" in Novice, Intermediate, and Advanced, or when the handler calls \"Finish\" in Excellent level.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"8 Search Rules – General","t":"8.8 Reinforcers","h":"<p>Handlers may take reinforcers into the search area or leave them on an elevated platform outside of the search area specified by the judge, which will be the same for all participants.</p><ul><li>a) Reinforcers (food or toys) may be visible.</li><li>b) Food must either be contained in a pocket/treat pouch if carried, or in a closed container outside the search area at the place specified by the judge.</li><li>c) If the reinforcers/rewards are left outside the search area, the dog must leave the search area under control and may not be sent ahead of the handler.</li><li>d) If, in the judge's opinion, the dog has not left the search area in a controlled manner, a major fault may be applied.</li><li>e) Reinforcers may be used at any point during the search.</li><li>f) Handlers must not contaminate the search area with their reinforcer. No food or toys should be thrown or dropped within or adjacent to the search area.</li><li>g) If, in the judge's opinion, the search area has been contaminated a major fault will be deducted.</li><li>h) Handlers must not damage the search area or anything within the search area when reinforcing their dog.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"9 Faults","t":"9.1 Trial failure (score of 0)","h":"<p>Faults are applied based on the following regulations/criteria. Trial failure incurs a score of 0 and applies for:</p><ul><li>a) If the time for the search element has run out, points will be gained only for found hides in Intermediate levels and above. If no hides are found the team will lose all the points for that trial.</li><li>b) If the handler requests help from the judge or aborts the search, the team will lose all points for that search.</li><li>c) Fouling: The dog vomits, urinates, or defecates at any time while searching.</li><li>d) Handler fails to call alert in the allocated time.</li><li>e) Working the dog off lead.</li><li>f) If, in the opinion of the judge, harsh or intimidating handling has taken place they may deduct all points for that search.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"9 Faults","t":"9.2a Incorrect alert call","h":"<p>Faults are deducted according to categories of fault type - incorrect alert call, major and minor.</p><ul><li>i) An incorrect alert call (false alert) will be a non-qualifying score.</li><li>ii) At Intermediate level and above, calling alert on an already found hide will incur a deduction of three points the first time it happens.</li><li>iii) At Intermediate level and above, the judge will call \"Already found - continue\" and the team may continue the search. If the hide is called for a third time the search will end and will be a non-qualifying score.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"9 Faults","t":"9.2b Major faults (3 points)","h":"<p>Major faults incur a deduction of 3 points and are as follows:</p><ul><li>i) Damage to the search area: including, but not limited to excessive pawing, digging, or interacting with the containers which require the replacement of one or more containers or grill covers.</li><li>ii) Interacting with containers which results in spillage of contents.</li><li>iii) Compromising the search area: handler's reward delivery has jeopardized the integrity of the search area by damage or contamination from toys or food.</li><li>iv) Dropping food or toy rewards at any time during the search.</li><li>v) Leaving the search area. The team (both handler and dog) leaves the search area during a search.</li><li>vi) Inappropriate fouling in the immediate vicinity of the search area: including but not limited to marking on nearby trees, defecating in the vicinity.</li><li>vii) Scattering or throwing of food or toys in the immediate vicinity of the search area.</li><li>viii) Safety issues or other infractions that may place the dog, handler, judge, or stewards in a potentially dangerous situation.</li><li>ix) Failing to call finish in Excellent level.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"9 Faults","t":"9.2c Minor faults (2 points)","h":"<p>Minor Faults incur a deduction of 2 points. Faults will be given for:</p><ul><li>a) Tangling the line.</li><li>b) Dropping the line.</li><li>c) Handler errors.</li><li>d) Neither the handler nor the dog crosses the start line.</li></ul><p>The judge's decision is final. No challenge or argument directly with the judge will be tolerated.</p>"},{"d":"b","g":"10 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"10.1 General entry requirements","h":"<p>All entry criteria must be met for a team to participate in a SSANZ Buried Hide trial.</p><ul><li>a) All handlers at a SSANZ Buried Hide Trial must be current financial members of SSANZ.</li><li>b) Handlers under the age of 16 are considered youth handlers (See Addendum 1).</li><li>c) To enter an Intermediate Buried Hide Trial a dog must have a Novice Buried Hide Title.</li><li>d) To enter an Advanced Buried Hide Trial a dog must have an Intermediate Buried Hide Title.</li><li>e) To enter an Excellent Buried Hide Trial a dog must have an Advanced Buried Hide Title.</li><li>f) Once a dog has a title at any particular Buried Hide level, the dog can no longer trial at that level. Exceptions are: i. Excellent level, where a team can continue to trial after gaining their title. ii. When a dog is entered on both days at a double trial and qualifies on the first day at the trial to receive their title they are permitted to run at the same level on the second day. iii. When a dog has entered a trial and after entries have closed they qualify out of their current level, they may trial at the level for which they have already entered. iv. However, if the Organisation is also offering the next level up the handler may request to trial at the higher level. The decision will rest with the Organisation as to whether they can accommodate the request.</li><li>g) Handlers may only enter one dog at each level at a trial.</li><li>h) A judge may not enter a dog in the specific trial they are judging.</li><li>i) A judge who is judging a trial may handle a dog in another trial at the same event at the discretion of the organiser.</li><li>j) Dogs may trial with an alternate handler to their owner and any qualifying certificates achieved will count towards the dog's title.</li><li>k) Organisers and officials may participate in a trial at the discretion of the organisation provided it does not impede the smooth running of the trial. The handler must not have been involved in the preparation of the search area in any capacity.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"10 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"10.2 Requirements for the Handler","h":"<ul><li>a) Have not been banned or excluded from trialling with SSANZ.</li><li>b) Enclosed, secure footwear must be worn at all times. A handler who presents with insecure footwear will be asked to rectify the problem. If no attempt is made to rectify, the handler may be asked to leave the trial.</li><li>c) Training vests, bags, hats etc., may be worn during searches.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"10 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"10.3 Requirements for the dog","h":"<ul><li>a) All dogs participating in SSANZ Buried Hide trials must be registered with SSANZ.</li><li>b) Over the age of six months.</li><li>c) Dogs attending the trial should be clean. Dogs who are excessively dirty will be excluded from trialling that day.</li><li>d) Dogs who raise health concerns for the judge may be excluded if a veterinary certificate is not available.</li><li>e) A veterinary certificate giving a brief outline and stating that a dog is fit to trial is required if any injury/disability/illness is obvious. A vet release form is available from SSANZ on the scentsports.nz website for completion prior to the trial.</li><li>f) Dogs must have a current vaccination record, issued by a registered professional.</li><li>g) Dogs must have a current registration with their Local Authority.</li><li>h) Bitches in season are not allowed to participate, nor permitted at the venue where the trial is held.</li><li>i) Notification is required to be given to the organisation at the earliest indication that a bitch may be in season at the time of the trial.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"10 Trial Entry Criteria","t":"10.4 Menacing dogs","h":"<ul><li>a) A dog that has been classified as menacing or dangerous under the Dog Control Act (1996), may participate in a SSANZ Trial at the discretion of the hosting organisation.</li><li>b) Additional information must be provided to the organiser on request. This can include, but is not limited to: (i) Owner name; (ii) Owner address; (iii) Dog name; (iv) Dog breed; (v) Dog colour; (vi) Type of classification.</li><li>c) A copy of the exemption document must be provided with the entry form.</li><li>d) A dog that has been classified as menacing under the Dog Control Act (1996), and which has been granted an exemption under Section 33E (5) of the Dog Control Act (1996) by the local territorial authority that has jurisdiction over the trial location, may participate at a SSANZ Trial within the parameters of the exemption.</li><li>e) SSANZ organisations may require dogs with a menacing classification to be muzzled at all times, even if they have an exemption.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"11 Equipment","t":"11 Equipment","h":"<ul><li>a) Lead length and type is at the handler's discretion.</li><li>b) Leads can be attached to a suitable harness or collar for the searches as defined in these regulations. Correctly fitted non-restrictive harnesses are permitted for use in SSANZ trials.</li><li>c) If the line is to be attached to the harness it must be affixed to the top (spine), not to any front clip that may be present.</li><li>d) Check, choke, slip, prong, citronella collars, head halters, and E collars are not permitted in the search areas.</li><li>e) Optional equipment worn by the dog such as coats, boots, or muzzles other than those outlined above, may be permitted at the discretion of the judge, and permission will not be unreasonably withheld.</li><li>f) Belly bands or incontinence nappies are permitted. If a dog lifts its hind leg or squats as if to pee while searching it will still be faulted as a fouling incident.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"12 Trial Protocol","t":"12.1–12.7 Trial protocol","h":"<ul><li>12.1 No unofficial odour may be brought anywhere onsite, other than in the confines of a private vehicle.</li><li>12.2 A briefing will be given before the trial starts and this is mandatory for all handlers to attend. a) Out of bounds areas will be outlined at the trial briefing. b) Search areas will be defined. Once defined, it is the responsibility of the handlers to stay out of these areas unless specifically exempted by an official on the day. c) Health and safety details for the venue will be advised. d) General housekeeping and details of the trial will be covered.</li><li>12.3 Search areas will be defined visually and may also be broadly outlined during the briefing. a) Handlers are not permitted to enter the search areas until it is their turn to trial, unless authorised by the judge or delegated official. b) If a handler enters the search area without authorisation they may be disqualified from participating at the trial.</li><li>12.4 Handlers are prohibited from discussing any detail in relation to the search or the operation of the search with any other participant or spectator until all the teams have concluded that search.</li><li>12.5 If, in the opinion of the judge, harsh or intimidating handling has taken place, they may deduct all points for that search.</li><li>12.6 The judge or organiser may ask a trialist to leave if it is deemed necessary in the interests of the safety and welfare of other dogs, handlers, stewards and/or judges, or due to bad sportsmanship, including but not limited to disrespectful treatment of the venue, volunteers, officials, or fellow trialists, and intentional disclosure/knowledge of the hide locations.</li><li>12.7 If a handler has any concerns regarding the running of the trial or regarding any set up of a search area/judging matter, they must in the first instance bring up their concern with the Organiser. If the issue can not be resolved by the Organisation, members can complete a Feedback form which can be found on the SSANZ website and Facebook page.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"12 Trial Protocol","t":"12.8 Recording of the event","h":"<p><strong>12.8.1 Recording of event - Handler</strong></p><ul><li>a) A handler may wear and use visible head or chest-mounted video equipment.</li><li>b) Recordings of any search must not be shared with anyone until the search has finished.</li><li>c) Recordings cannot be used to challenge the judge's decision.</li><li>d) Use of any recording equipment must not delay the start of any search and the judge and organisation reserve the right to refuse set up of recording equipment.</li><li>e) A handler may request permission to have a separate person record their search and the following will apply: i) Permission will be granted at the discretion of the organisation and judge. ii) Any person recording a search must follow all directions given by the judge or designated official. iii) The judge or designated official reserves the right to ask a person recording searches to leave if they believe that is appropriate.</li></ul><p><strong>12.8.2 Recording of event - Organisation</strong></p><ul><li>a) An organisation may provide a videographer for each search.</li><li>b) If any recording is to occur at a trial, this must be advised by the organisation at the time of trial entry or at the briefing.</li><li>c) Handlers must be given the choice, at the time of trial entry, to agree or refuse any photos or video being taken of them, their dog, and/or their searches.</li><li>d) With permission from the handler, photos or video may be used by the Organisation for the purpose of sharing on social media, future advertising, and/or with sponsors, and must be made available to the handler in question on request.</li><li>e) If a professional photographer is at the venue, photos and/or video may be available to purchase and may only be shared with permission of the subject of the photo or video.</li><li>f) Organisations are not responsible for recording each and every search.</li><li>g) Only the videographer(s) organised by the organisation may take photos or video of handlers, dogs and searches at the trial venue.</li><li>h) The handler, judge, or designated official reserves the right to ask the videographer to leave if they believe that is appropriate.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"13 Organisation or Host Requirements","t":"13.1–13.3 Hosting, advertising and ballots","h":"<ul><li>13.1 To host a SSANZ trial you must be a current financial SSANZ Organisational member. Organisers must apply to have a trial recognised as a SSANZ trial by: a) Emailing a completed application to hold a SSANZ trial with the SSANZ Secretary. b) Emailing the application at least three months prior to the trial date.</li><li>13.2 Organisations are responsible for providing the correct equipment and search areas as required by the SSANZ Buried Hide Regulations.</li><li>13.3 Upon approval, trial advertising must include the following information: a) \"This trial is being conducted under the rules and regulations of the Scent Sports Association of New Zealand (SSANZ)\". b) All trials must advertise a closing date, no less than 21 days prior to the trial date, and entries must be kept open until that date. c) Judge/s for each search and/or level. d) Any awards or prizes. e) If there is going to be a videographer or photographer in attendance, this should be advised on the trial flyer or at the start of the trial. f) Only once the entries have closed can ballots (if required) be carried out.</li><li>13.3 h) On the closing date for entries, team spots will be accepted in the following order: i) Judges and stewards (see definition) of the event will be accepted first. ii) Dogs that have less than three Buried Hide qualifying certificates. iii) Dogs that have between three and six (or more) Buried Hide qualifying certificates. iv) In the event of the organisation receiving more entries than available spaces, a random draw from all the remaining dogs will be conducted to fill the remaining spaces in the trial. v) If there is a ballot, teams that do not receive a place will be added to a wait list. The wait list order will be emailed to those who were unsuccessful after the ballot. Should a team withdraw their entry, handlers on the wait list will be offered the empty slot in order of 13.3.h above.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"13 Organisation or Host Requirements","t":"13.4–13.14 Organiser responsibilities","h":"<ul><li>13.4 SSANZ will not be responsible or held liable for any accident or injury that may occur at a trial. SSANZ encourages all Organisations to hold public liability insurance for their trials.</li><li>13.5 Organisers may run searches and levels in any order at a trial to suit their requirements, provided these meet the criteria outlined for each individual search.</li><li>13.6 The Organiser is responsible for contracting a SSANZ judge/judges. a) The Organiser and judge are to communicate between themselves directly and decide on any monetary or other remuneration. This is not a SSANZ responsibility.</li><li>13.7 Organisations are responsible for providing the correct equipment and search areas as required by the SSANZ Regulations, including odour infused Q-tips and hides/vessels. A raised table, chair, or other suitable platform must be available for handlers to place personal effects before the start of the search.</li><li>13.8 Entry fees charged to enter a trial are set by the Organiser. a) Entry fees are the property of the Organiser. b) Entry fees should be fair and reasonable to the trialists.</li><li>13.9 The organiser is responsible for obtaining official SSANZ qualifying certificates.</li><li>13.10 Organisers, at their discretion, may present additional participation certificates/awards, but these are not officially recognised by SSANZ for the purposes of any titles.</li><li>13.11 The Organiser may set a competitive aspect to a trial if they wish based on the following criteria: i) A non-faulted team with the fastest combined time of all their searches may be awarded Highest Qualifier. ii) Teams on no faults place above faulted teams. iii) If there are no un-faulted teams, then the fastest time will come into play after considering the lowest number of faults. iv) Awards and prizes are the responsibility of the Organiser.</li><li>13.12 Organisers may hold additional novelty scent games at SSANZ trials, with the understanding they are to be conducted in a manner that is consistent with SSANZ protocols.</li><li>13.13 Organisers must forward a copy of all results, including scores and search times to the SSANZ Secretary within 7 days of the conclusion of the trial.</li><li>13.14 Organisers must keep records of the trial for 12 months after the trial date including but not limited to: i) Application to hold a SSANZ trial. ii) Entry forms. iii) Menacing dog information and exemptions. iv) Financial records.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"14 Definitions","t":"Definitions (Buried Hides)","h":"<ul><li><strong>Alert:</strong> Word used by the handler to communicate to the judge that the dog has indicated the location of the odour.</li><li><strong>Change of behaviour:</strong> Any behaviour by the dog which makes it clear to the Judge and/or handler that the dog has encountered odour.</li><li><strong>Event:</strong> Several trials run in the same time frame at the same venue.</li><li><strong>Finish:</strong> The handler is required to call \"Finish\" when they believe all hides in the search area have been located and called in some levels. The \"Finish\" call stops the official search time. If \"Finish\" is not called, a maximum time will be allocated to that search, and only hides found will gain points.</li><li><strong>Handler:</strong> The person participating in the search with the dog.</li><li><strong>Judge:</strong> Official scorer at a trial, SSANZ approved judges.</li><li><strong>Locations:</strong> The area where the search is set up. A venue can have multiple locations for searches to be set up. Note – This is relevant for buried hide trials only.</li><li><strong>Level:</strong> One specific level of a SSANZ Buried Hide trial e.g., Novice.</li><li><strong>Official:</strong> Any person commissioned by the organiser to help run a trial.</li><li><strong>Organiser:</strong> A current financial Organisation member of SSANZ.</li><li><strong>Owner:</strong> The registered owner of a dog.</li><li><strong>Search:</strong> One element of a level, e.g., water search.</li><li><strong>Start Line:</strong> A clear line marked by cones or other items that indicate where the search will begin. The team is expected to start the search by crossing the line. A minor fault will be deducted if neither the dog nor the handler crosses the line. Time begins when any part of the team enters the search area.</li><li><strong>Steward:</strong> The person who is working with a Judge to brief and/or time keep at any element.</li><li><strong>Team:</strong> A dog and handler.</li><li><strong>Trial:</strong> One single element, water and/or sand, at any level.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"Addenda","t":"Addendum 1 – Youth Handler Policy (Buried Hides)","h":"<p>Handlers younger than 16 years of age can trial at SSANZ trials with the following:</p><ul><li>Be a member of SSANZ.</li><li>Must have an adult guardian accompany and be responsible for the youth and the dog at the trial.</li><li>As the youth team requires the adult guardian to support them at the individual elements of the trial if the adult is also trialling in the same level the adult is to be placed earlier than the youth in the running orders.</li></ul><p>The guardian is to be nominated with the entry. A statement is completed as part of the entry as to who is the adult guardian responsible for the team acknowledging the below statement:</p><p><em>I acknowledge that under the Dog Control Act 1996 I am legally responsible for the dog's control and compliance. I will remain on site and within line-of-sight during all runs, carry a leash, and ensure compliance with all council bylaws and event rules.</em></p><p>If it has been noted on the entry form that the dog is reactive the guardian must accompany the team at all times.</p><p>When the guardian goes to the search areas, they:</p><ul><li>Must stand where instructed and not impact any of the search areas.</li><li>Cannot speak or move while a team is searching.</li><li>Will not coach the team between or during elements.</li></ul>"},{"d":"b","g":"Addenda","t":"Addendum 2 – Photography Guidelines (Buried Hides)","h":"<p>The Buried Hide Photography Guidelines are the same as Addendum 2 of the Nosework Regs, except that they refer to Regulation 12.8 for handler recording, and say handlers must be given the choice <em>at the time of trial entry</em> to agree or refuse photos or video.</p><p>Photographers are required to remain outside the search area and should ask the judge where they would like them to stand for the duration of the search. A request to bring someone else in may be declined if the space is crowded or not conducive to recording. If a judge declines or asks a friend to leave, they must comply immediately without further discussion. Official photographers must not interfere with the search, distract the dog or handler, or provide any clue as to the location of any hide(s).</p><p>Recommended best practice (12 points): ask the organisation about teams who don't want to be photographed; liaise with judges before the briefing on whether and where to stand (the judge's decision is final); be visible from the start line; finalise camera settings before teams come through; disable shutter noise; attend the handler briefing; minimise movement and chatter (never say \"call it\"); if a dog is distracted, lower the camera and break eye contact; plan for safety with reactive dogs; plan routes between areas; never enter the search area without the judge's express permission; check with the handler before using any image beyond what was agreed.</p>"}]},"bts":{"modified":1794988933000,"builtIn":true,"sections":[{"g":"About Beyond the Sniff","t":"About Beyond the Sniff","h":"<p>This document is intended to provide guidance to Judges, addressing questions or situations that may arise that may not be not explicitly covered by SSANZ Nosework Trial Regulations.  </p>"},{"g":"Code of Conduct for SSANZ Nosework Trial Judges","t":"Purpose","h":"<p>The purpose of this document is to provide a Code of Conduct for SSANZ trainees and approved Judges. </p>"},{"g":"Code of Conduct for SSANZ Nosework Trial Judges","t":"SSANZ Judges Code of Conduct","h":"<ul><li>Judges shall remember that they are ambassadors for SSANZ and must always act with courtesy, professional integrity, and impartiality towards all those involved with SSANZ, via all modes of communication including social media platforms.</li><li>Judges shall demonstrate a comprehensive knowledge of the SSANZ Rules and Regulations as pertains to setting up all trial searches, including how to manage safety in and around search areas</li><li>Judges must evaluate each team they judge impartially, and award each team the appropriate points for each search based on the Rules and Regulations of SSANZ.</li><li>Judges shall not offer any training advice while judging nor turn the element/s they are judging into a training session while the trial is in progress.</li><li>Judges should be considerate when commenting on results sheets, always appreciating that individuals may take comments literally.</li><li>Any issue arising during the course of a trial should be brought to the attention of the organisers as soon as practical so that appropriate steps may be taken to address the concern. In the event that the issue is not able to be resolved at the time, the next step would be to submit a full report to the SSANZ Committee.</li><li>Except in unavoidable circumstances, Judges shall be punctual when fulfilling judging assignments.</li><li>If a Judge is proved to be in breach of any animal welfare laws they will be dismissed from the panel.</li></ul><p>Any reports of a Judge deemed to be in breach of this Code of Conduct shall be investigated by a subcommittees selected by the SSANZ Committee. Members of the subcommittee will take into account all evidence and the Judge concerned will have the right to present their case.</p><p>In the event the Judge is deemed to have breached the Code of Conduct, the subcommittee will submit a report to the SSANZ Committee that will recommend a course of action to be taken. Possible actions include but are not limited to:</p><ul><li>A mentorship set up with a senior Judge and the Judge concerned.</li><li>A warning letter issued outlining steps that will be taken in the event of a similar breach in the future. </li><li>A demotion, with a requirement to complete a set number of trainee judging appointments (stewarding with intent and/or probationary judging) before being considered for reinstatement.</li><li>Dismissal from the judging panel and potentially from SSANZ in accordance with the Constitution.</li></ul>"},{"g":"SSANZ Hide Setting","t":"SSANZ Hide Setting","h":"<p><img data-key=\"kix.urlsmugf381f\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"573\" height=\"749\"></p><p>When deciding on your hide location, imagine the WORST possible scenario of airflow.  If that happens, will your hide still work according to these principles?</p><p><img data-key=\"kix.k1a44owy2bc4\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"391\" height=\"214\"></p><p><b>It's the judge’s responsibility to make the hide sourceable - from dog 1 to dog 50.</b></p><p><b>A</b>CCESS - How many access points are there to this hide? </p><p><b>S</b>TRUCTURE - Is there sufficient structure around the hide that will make it solvable? </p><p><b>D</b>OG / <b>D</b>ECISION - Does the placement / structure enable the dog to make a good decision on this hide? </p><p><b>A</b>IRFLOW - In the worst possible airflow conditions will this hide still work?</p>"},{"g":"SSANZ Nosework Levels & Progression","t":"SSANZ Nosework Levels & Progression","h":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><b>Level</b></p></td><td><p><b>No. of Searches</b></p></td><td><p><b>Total </b></p><p><b>No. of Hides</b></p></td><td><p><b>Odours</b></p></td><td><p><b>Detail</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Novice</b></p></td><td><p>4</p></td><td><p>4</p></td><td><p>Clove Bud</p></td><td><ul><li>1 False alert allowed</li><li>Basic odour recognition</li><li>The hide should be on the dog’s natural path around the search area.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Intermediate</b></p></td><td><p>4</p></td><td><p>4</p></td><td><p>Sweet Birch</p></td><td><ul><li>0 False alerts allowed</li><li>Still basic odour recognition</li><li>The odour plume should be on the dog’s natural path around the search area.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Advanced</b></p></td><td><p>4</p></td><td><p>8</p></td><td><p>Clove Bud, Sweet Birch or a combination of both</p></td><td><ul><li>0 False alerts allowed</li><li>Testing the skills of moving on from a found hide and finding another one</li><li>Odour plumes should be on the dog’s natural path around the search area</li><li>Non-food distractor introduced in containers</li><li>Convergence should be minimal, if at all.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Excellent</b></p></td><td><p>5</p></td><td><p>12</p></td><td><p>Clove Bud, Sweet Birch and Cypress or a combination of 2</p></td><td><ul><li>0 False alerts allowed</li><li>Stamina is required to work 12 hides with up to three hides per search</li><li>All hides are accessible (unless for height) and odour plumes should be encountered on the dog’s natural path around the search area</li><li>May encounter closer hides than in Advanced</li><li>Food distractions introduced in containers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Premium</b></p></td><td><p>6</p></td><td><p>15</p></td><td><p>Clove Bud, Sweet Birch and Cypress or a combination of 2</p></td><td><ul><li>0 False alerts allowed</li><li>May include inaccessible hides</li><li>Searches will have unknown number of hides (no blank searches)</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Elite</b></p></td><td><p>6</p></td><td><p>15-18</p></td><td><p>Clove Bud, Sweet Birch and Cypress or a combination of 2</p></td><td><ul><li>Will include a blank search and unknown number of hides</li><li>A false alert results in point deductions</li><li>Calling the blank search correctly earns double points</li><li>Search areas may be more complex</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Ultimate</b></p></td><td><p>6-8</p></td><td><p>TBD</p></td><td><p>Clove Bud, Sweet Birch and Cypress or a combination of 2</p></td><td><ul><li>Held over 2 days with a competitive element</li><li>May have a blank search on both days and hide numbers will be unknown.</li></ul></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Safety & Welfare Risks","t":"Removal of a Team","h":"<p>Regulation clause 13.8 states:</p><p><i>The Judge or Organiser may ask a trialist to leave if it is deemed necessary in the interests of the safety and welfare of other dogs, handlers, stewards, and/or judges, …</i></p><p><br>A single incident that suggests there may be a risk to any of these should be sufficient to require a compassionate conversation with the person involved. We advise that you involve the Organisational Representative, and look at it through a lens of &quot;what is in the best interest of the dog and of others attending the trial?”.  Dogs with big feelings are often struggling, and being in confined spaces and around lots of other dogs may cause a reaction with a detrimental outcome.</p>"},{"g":"Safety & Welfare Risks","t":"Safe Hide Placement","h":"<p>Common sense should guide everything that we do. When appraising a search area, you should always consider what risks exist and how you might mitigate them. This would include avoiding the placement of a hide on or in an electrical outlet (risk of electrocution), or poison bait station (risk of poisoning). </p>"},{"g":"Safety & Welfare Risks","t":"Safe Search Area","h":"<p>Take time when setting up your search area to review it for potential hazards. Examples might include discarded food wrappers, cigarette butts, metal shavings, broken glass. Where possible, clean these away to the best of your ability. Remember that just using cones to section off an area will not prevent a dog from moving across the barrier in search of odour. If the hazard is significant and / or unmanageable, seek assistance from the trial organiser.</p>"},{"g":"Safety & Welfare Risks","t":"If an Accident Happens","h":"<p>Despite your best efforts, an accident may still happen in your search area. Your role in the first instance is to ensure that everyone is OK. Above all, be kind. If a team has been involved, consider offering them a re-run either immediately or later in the day. Take steps to prevent the same issue from being repeated and ensure that the organiser is informed of the incident in a timely manner. We can’t offer guidance for every situation, however if you approach the situation with kindness and common sense, you will have managed everything to the best of your ability.</p>"},{"g":"Safety & Welfare Risks","t":"Reinforcers and Safety","h":"<p>The regulations set out a number of factors that are important for Judges to take into account in regard to placement of reinforcers outside the search area. These are in place to minimise risk to judges and other helpers, and should be applied consistently across all teams, regardless of the dog’s size or perceived danger.  </p><p><i>Section 9.8</i></p><ol><li><i>Handlers may take their reinforcers into the search area or leave them on an elevated location outside of the search area. When left outside the search area, the location will be specified by the judge and be the same for all participants. </i></li><li><i>Food must be contained either in a pocket/treat pouch if carried, or in a closed container outside the search area at the location specified. </i></li><li><i>If the reinforcers/rewards are left outside the search area, the dog must leave the search area under control and may not be sent ahead of the handler.</i></li><li><i>If, in the Judge’s opinion, the dog has not left the search area in a controlled manner, a major fault may be applied.</i></li></ol><p>Note - elevated means raised, such as on a table, chair, or other suitable surface.</p><p>Any safety issue that may place the dog, handler, judge or steward in a potentially dangerous situation is considered a major fault and should incur 5 penalty points.</p>"},{"g":"Non-Compliant Equipment","t":"Non-Compliant Equipment","h":"<p>SSANZ sanctioned trials should be conducted according to our rules and regulations. These are not merely “guidelines”, they are requirements. While the primary responsibility to supply compliant search areas, vehicles and other equipment lies with the organisation, the judge has ultimate responsibility to ensure that search areas and equipment used are compliant. In the event that there are concerns, this is the process that must be followed.</p><p><img data-key=\"kix.p89vmrjhs4pp\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"594\" height=\"709\"></p>"},{"g":"Search Area Becomes Unsuitable","t":"Search Area Becomes Unsuitable","h":"<p>On occasion, factors within the search area might change, or something new may come to light, that causes you to review the suitability of the area with regard to the safety and welfare of the teams participating in the trial. In some circumstances, a judge may need to pause the trial and discuss alternative arrangements with the host. </p><p>We are grateful to Adele Williams for creating another flowchart (see following page) on how judges might handle such search area challenges.  Examples might include:</p><ul><li>Prickles on grass in search area: If possible, relocate search area to a new location.  Allow any non-qualifiers to rerun if this occurs.  </li><li>Excessive fouling: Review options in ‘Fouling in the search area flowchart’.  Consider moving the search area or boundaries if possible and appropriate.  </li><li>Hot surface:  If the surface of the search area e.g. tar seal or concrete heats up to a point where this may be a health and safety concern for the comfort of the dogs, consider appropriate remedial measures as suggested above.  </li><li>Insect infestation:  If these pose a health hazard, attempt to remove them or exclude them by adjusting the boundary.  </li><li>Toxic plants:  Remove if possible or consider relocating search.  </li></ul><p>Distractions of a minor nature that are fair and equitable across all trialists should not necessarily warrant remedial action.  There may be events that occur that whilst not distracting to one dog are distracting to another, therefore it is not possible to determine all potential distractions and the Judge’s discretion on considering if a rerun is justified will be warranted on such occasions. </p><p><img data-key=\"kix.lixcg0zbt8hm\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"672\" height=\"895\"></p>"},{"g":"Contamination of the Search Area","t":"Contamination of the Search Area","h":"<p>As judges it is our responsibility to set deliberate odour puzzles for dogs to navigate and solve. And, as stated previously, the hide(s) that we set should ideally be available, accessible and unambiguous. Any contamination in or near the search area will result in, at best, a distraction, and at worst, misleading information being presented to the dog. The most often considered sources of contamination are from a handler dropping either food or toy, or from a dog fouling (see next page for a great resource for this). However there are many other potential sources of contamination, with odour being top of that list. </p>"},{"g":"Contamination of the Search Area","t":"Search Area Preparation","h":"<p>Where and how you prepare your hide(s), and where you store your kit are both potential sources of contamination. A dropped Q-tip is clearly going to result in residual odour where it falls, but have you also considered what happens when you walk through the search area with a prepared hide in your hand? Testing a hide location with a “loaded” hide only to find that it isn’t suitable, will lead to a hot spot in your search area. Opening your odour kit on a platform within the search area, is going to release a cloud of odour in that location. One judge observed a high level of interest from dogs at the base of a door which led to a closed room in which her kit was stored. </p><ul><li>Always test your hide locations with a blank hide prior to setting out the odour.</li><li>Think carefully about where and how you will prepare your hide prior to placing it. In your situation, is it better to have a cloud of odour beside the hide location, or well away from the search area?</li><li>Store your odour kit carefully and preferably well away from your search area (not just out of sight).</li></ul>"},{"g":"Contamination of the Search Area","t":"Reuse of Search Areas","h":"<p>There will be occasions when an organisation requires you to use a search area that has already been used for another search over the course of the trial. This is never ideal as there will always be some lingering odour that is unproductive for the dogs to work and eliminate. There is also a risk that a previous hide has contaminated the spot where it was placed via absorption resulting in residual odour. A dog alerting on this would not be wrong. </p><p>SSANZ policy regarding reuse of areas is covered in an Addendum to the Regulations. Please ensure that you are familiar with this.</p>"},{"g":"Contamination of the Search Area","t":"Human Scent","h":"<p>Remember, we are constantly shedding a trail of dead cells, dander and more. Some dogs take this information into account when searching. Think about where you sit, and move around in the search area when you have to be in there, ie during set up. We always aim to avoid unnecessary movement during the search, and only follow teams from a distance when it is absolutely necessary.</p>"},{"g":"Contamination of the Search Area","t":"Dog Slobber","h":"<p>Dogs dribble—that’s a fact. When dogs search, they can leave behind saliva deposits containing cortisol, pheromones, and traces of recently eaten treats. These deposits may influence subsequent dogs and are often most noticeable on containers and vehicles, potentially affecting an alert call.</p><p>Whether you choose to wipe surfaces between runs is up to you. Wiping may remove some of this contamination although the picture will always differ from one run to the next and this cannot be fully controlled.</p><p>If you clean between runs, good odour hygiene is essential. Use a separate cloth or paper towel for the hide and for all other surfaces. Disposable paper towels help reduce the risk of contamination if a fresh one is used each time. After use, all “hot” cloths or paper towels should be placed in a closed container away from the search area. </p><p>With thanks to Adele for this flowchart on fouling in the search area.</p><p><img data-key=\"kix.kqshuhyn9u8s\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"640\" height=\"805\"></p>"},{"g":"Compromised Dog Flowchart","t":"Compromised Dog Flowchart","h":"<p>“Compromised” refers to both emotional and physical states. A trial environment is not necessarily appropriate for every dog.</p><p><img data-key=\"kix.jwejm586jcj7\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"544\" height=\"793\"></p>"},{"g":"Veterinary Release Forms","t":"Veterinary Release Forms","h":"<p>The regulations state that dogs attending a trial must be in a state of good general health. Dogs who raise health concerns may be excluded if a veterinary certificate is not available. Therefore, if you have occasion to doubt the general health or state of fitness for nosework of a participating dog, you have the authority to ask the handler and/or the organiser for further information and potentially exclude the team from the trial. SSANZ philosophy is that the wellbeing of a dog is far more important than a qualification. </p><p>A veterinary release form is available to handlers via the SSANZ website.</p>"},{"g":"Belly Bands and Incontinence Pants","t":"Belly Bands and Incontinence Pants","h":"<p>Dogs in SSANZ trials may wear belly bands or incontinence pants, according to clause 12 f), however, if the dog lifts its leg or squats as if to urinate they are still to be penalised as per regulations:</p><p><i>10.1 Element failure.</i></p><p><i>d) Fouling. If the dog vomits, urinates, or defecates at any time while searching the search will end and the team will lose all points for that search.</i></p><p>Examples of belly bands and incontinence pants can be seen in the images below.  </p><p>IMPORTANT - in-season bitches are excluded from attending a trial, as per the regulations.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.g5ht2d8gb0a0\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"190\" height=\"173\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.js2hu6o9bj4t\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"173\" height=\"173\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.q2b15fpt4ysu\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"173\" height=\"173\"></p></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Collars and Leashes","t":"Collars and Leashes","h":"<p>SSANZ regulations restrict the use of specific pieces of equipment.  <br>Refer section<i> 12 d)    Check, choke, slip, prong, </i><i>citronella collars</i><i>, head halters, and E-collars are not permitted in the search areas. </i>This does mean that a dog may be brought to the start line wearing one of these, and not be penalised as long as it is removed for the duration of the search.  </p><p>IMPORTANT - citronella is an essential oil, and, although not specifically regulated against, units designed to emit citronella as a deterrent to barking, ought to be left in the handler’s personal vehicle. If you do notice one on a dog’s collar, please have a kind word with the handler to advise them of the risk.</p><p>Flexi or retractable leads are permitted.  Refer section <i>12 a)  Lead length and type is at the handler’s discretion.</i></p><p><img data-key=\"kix.nj76e7evu7bq\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"200\" height=\"129\"></p>"},{"g":"Search Area Boundaries","t":"Search Area Boundaries","h":"<p>Questions have been raised regarding the interpretation of “in the vicinity” as used in Regulation point 10.2 b) Major Faults.  <br><i>vi)     Fouling in the vicinity of the search area: including, but not limited to, marking on nearby</i></p><p><i>        trees or defecating in the vicinity.</i></p><p><i>vii)   Scattering or throwing of food or toys in the immediate vicinity of the search area.</i></p><p>This is to be at the judge’s discretion, and will be specific to the search area concerned. We advise that you mentally identify your “contamination free zone” during set-up. The area decided upon should take into account whether any contamination might cause a distraction for subsequent teams. This zone should be applied consistently for all teams, regardless of whether contamination is by food or toy, or by the team’s place in the running order.  </p><p>For example, a tree or retaining wall outside but in the same area as an exterior search area might be considered “in the vicinity”, whereas where there is a clear threshold, with a briefing area inside a door way, the corridor leading up to the room may be considered as outside the immediate vicinity.</p><p>Note - penalties are still applicable after the search has completed and until the team exits your predetermined “contamination free zone”.</p>"},{"g":"Start Line and Timing","t":"Start Line and Timing","h":"<p>The judge is required to set a start line for each search. Teams are expected to start the search by crossing the line. A minor fault should be deducted if neither the dog nor the handler crosses the line.</p><p>Please remember to brief your time steward that they should start the clock as soon as either member of the team enters the search area, regardless of if they cross the start line, or not.</p>"},{"g":"Photography/Videography at a Trial","t":"Photography/Videography at a Trial","h":"<p>As a courtesy, handlers should confirm with you, the judge, that you are happy for them to record their search. This may include bringing a friend through with them to capture video on their behalf.</p><p>When an organisation has a pre-arranged photographer, they should ensure that the photographer has seen a copy of the <a href=\"https://www.scentsports.nz/filedownload.php?type=4&amp;fileid=34\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SSANZ Photography Guidelines</a>. This outlines how a photographer should work with each judge to ensure that they are not distracting teams or disturbing the search area. If a team is marked as having a reactive dog, please check before they commence their run if an official photographer may remain.</p><p>Please work with any photographer or videographer (whether official or a handler’s friend), as far as practical. They should only enter the search area with express permission from you to reach a designated place, and they should never follow a team as they search. If you prefer not to, or if the search area is not conducive to having someone else present, you may decline any such request. </p>"},{"g":"Personal Judging Record Sheet & Debrief Video","t":"Personal Judging Record Sheet & Debrief Video","h":"<p>You are required to maintain a record of all SSANZ searches that you judge. If you do not already have a link to your Personal Judging Record Sheet, please request it from <a href=\"mailto:ssanzjdsubcommittee@gmail.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ssanzjdsubcommittee@gmail.com</a>. There is a column on there for you to save photos and/or a link to a video.<br><br>Often once a search has completed, we are in a rush to pack away and move on to our next commitment. However, recording a debrief video does not take long and is a powerful practice that will make you a better judge as you reflect and learn. There are several examples of great video reviews that have been shared on the Judges Facebook page. Don’t worry if it is not “slick”. The more you do, the better you will get and the record is for your own reflection, not public consumption, although we would love you to share them on Facebook too. 😊</p><p>Things to include in a debrief video are:</p><ul><li><b>Trial data</b> - date, organisation, venue, level and element</li><li><b>Search details</b> - search area and boundaries. Choice of hide location (ASDA), odour, number of Q-tips and vessel for each placement.</li><li><b>Environmental conditions</b> - patterns of heat, humidity, airflow, architecture, surfaces etc</li><li><b>Observations</b> - what worked or didn’t. What changed and by how much. How dogs worked it. Number of teams judged and pass rate. Possible reasons for missed hides.</li></ul>"},{"g":"Guidelines for Judging - General","t":"Guidelines for Judging - General","h":"<p>Hide setting is just one part of being a SSANZ judge. While we all need to perfect the art of setting hides that present an odour picture that is relatively stable and consistent across the running order, there is more to judging than just that.</p><p>Remember, to remain vigilant until teams leave your predetermined “contamination free zone”. You should only fault a team if you see something with your own eyes. A steward pointing out a dropped piece of food after a team has left, for example, is just something to tidy up. No faults may be allocated unless you observe the incident.</p><p>For all levels, if there is anything that you are unsure of, please check the current regulations or discuss it with another experienced judge. If you are unable to find the answer in a timely manner, the question that you should be asking is, &quot;does this pose a significant risk (to dog or people), or is there a high probability that it will lead to contamination or disturbance of the search area?&quot;  If the answer is no, then our recommendation is that you allow things to proceed, and follow up later, when you have time. While not ideal, there is an onus on the handler to also be familiar with the regulations, so if it is subsequently found to be contrary to the regulations, faults or a disqualification may be applied in retrospect.</p><p>If you move anything to make your search area more interesting or easier to navigate, please remember to put everything back as you found it before leaving. This includes closing any windows or doors that you open. Taking photos before moving things around can be a useful reference.</p>"},{"g":"Guidelines for Judging - General","t":"Responsibility for Search Area","h":"<p>Judges hold overall responsibility for everything that happens within their allocated search area, from when the organiser advises that you can start setting up, through all teams being judged, a debrief video has been completed, and everything has been packed away. No-one should enter the search area during that time unless with your explicit permission. When you are ready, you should advise the organiser that the search area is cleared and responsibility passes back to them. <b>Please do not allow people to re-enter the search area</b>. <b>The organiser may have a reason that they do not want foot traffic in that area.</b></p><p>The following pages summarise the key points for judging at each SSANZ level, and highlights important criteria for each of the elements.  All judges should be conversant with these when judging a trial.</p>"},{"g":"Guidelines for Novice - Trial Checklist","t":"Guidelines for Novice - Trial Checklist","h":"<p><b>Please check the current regulations if you are unsure of anything or discuss it with another experienced judge.</b></p><p>The purpose of the Novice trial is to test whether the dog can locate the target odour in a novel location within the time allotted and the handler can recognise the dog’s communication when the dog is at source/in the yes zone.</p><p>It should be a basic search with the hide placed in a location where odour is available and be accessible, i.e. the dog can get his/her nose onto the placement of the hide, (excluding containers).</p><p>The hide should <i><b>not</b></i> be placed where:</p><ul><li>It is difficult to access because of obstacles in the way.</li><li>It is difficult for the dog to get to source.</li><li>There is significant air movement that will cause the odour to disperse or pool rapidly.</li></ul><p>This level is not intended to present complex odour challenges and odour should be available, accessible, and unambiguous.</p><ul><li>Each search has <b>ONE HIDE: </b>Oil of Clove Bud.</li><li>Vessel/hide for odour is to be no larger than 50 ml capacity. The hide must have vent holes.</li><li>The hide must be accessible except for containers, where the nature of containers does not allow it to be accessible.</li><li>The dog should not be able to bite/ remove and/or ingest the vessel, nor should the hide be visible to the handler or the dog. </li><li>Every element must have a clear start line and clear search parameters.</li></ul><p><b>CONTAINERS </b>Time allowed is 2 minutes, set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>10 to 15 containers, of which one is a hot container.</li><li>The containers are to be set in a geometrical configuration. </li><li>The start line should be no less than one metre from the first container.</li><li>Boundaries should be clearly identifiable.</li><li>The containers will be uniform size with a minimum width of 210mm and maximum width of 600mm.</li><li>The containers shall be no more than 400mm in height.</li><li>There is to be a minimum distance of 750mm and a maximum distance of 1500mm between containers. If the containers are in rows there is to be a minimum distance of 900mm and a maximum distance of 1500mm between rows.</li><li>There should be additional spare containers, including a spare hot container, in case any need to be replaced.</li><li>The spare additional containers should not be in eyesight of the search area, and the hot container must be separated from the cold containers.</li></ul><p><b>INTERIOR</b> Time allowed is 3 minutes, set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>The area to be searched should be a minimum of 60sqm and no larger than 100sqm, in a single room.</li><li>It can be staged (having additional “stuff” put into the area) or left as you have found it “natural”.</li><li>To calculate the area of the search use the external boundaries of the search area. </li><li>The maximum height for placement of the hide is 650 mm.</li></ul><p><b>EXTERIOR</b> Time allowed is 3 minutes, set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>The area to be searched is to be a minimum area of 75sqm and no larger area than 200 sqm.</li><li>It can be staged (having additional “stuff” put into the area) or left as you have found it “natural”.</li><li>To calculate the area of the search use the external boundaries of the search area.</li><li>The maximum height for placement of hide is 650 mm.</li></ul><p><b>VEHICLE</b> Time allowed is 3 minutes set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>1 Vehicle which can be either a car, ute, or van.</li><li>Boundaries should be clearly identifiable.</li><li>The vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>Maximum height of hide on a vehicle is 650 mm, and it is to be no more than 100 mm deep.</li><li>The hide must NOT be placed inside the vehicle.</li><li>Suitable hide placements on a vehicle are underneath the towbar, number plates, in the wheel arch or wheel hub. </li><li>When placing a hide on a vehicle, think about placement to minimise dogs having to put their paws on the vehicle to avoid the possibility of the vehicle being scratched. </li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><b>CHECKLIST</b></p></td><td><p><b>YES</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><ul><li>Have you measured your search area?</li><li>Are there clear parameters for the handler to see what area is in play?</li><li>Have you determined the boundaries of your contamination-free zone?</li></ul></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you measured and checked the height of your hide, plus the depth on the vehicle?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you checked your search area for possible dangers or hazards?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you checked that your hide is not visible to handlers? <br>Do this with a cold hide before placing the hot hide out.</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>REMEMBER TO REMAIN VIGILANT FOR POTENTIAL FAULTS UNTIL A TEAM LEAVES YOUR <br>PREDETERMINED CONTAMINATION FREE ZONE.</b></p></td><td></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Guidelines for Intermediate - Trial Checklist","t":"Guidelines for Intermediate - Trial Checklist","h":"<p>The purpose of an Intermediate trial is to test whether the dog can locate the target odour in a novel location within the time allotted and the handler can recognise the dog’s communication when the dog is at source/in the yes zone.</p><p>It should be a basic search with the hide placed in a location where odour is available and accessible, <br>ie the dog can get his/her nose onto the placement of the hide (excluding containers).</p><p>The challenge compared to Novice is that the handler cannot have any incorrect calls, areas are slightly larger, containers will be variable in size and there will be two vehicles of any sort.</p><p>The hide should <i><b>not</b></i> be placed where:</p><ul><li>It is difficult to access because of obstacles in the way.</li><li>It is difficult for the dog to get to source.</li><li>Where there is significant air movement that will cause the odour to disperse or pool rapidly.</li></ul><p>This level is not intended to present complex odour challenges and odour should be available, accessible, and unambiguous. </p><ul><li>Each search has <b>ONE HIDE: </b>Sweet Birch.</li><li>Vessel/hide for odour is to be no larger than 50 ml capacity. The hide must have vent holes.</li><li>The hide must be accessible except for containers, where the nature of containers does not allow it to be accessible.</li><li>The dog should not be able to bite/ remove and/or ingest the vessel, nor should the hide be visible to the handler or the dog. </li><li>Every element must have a clear start line and clear search parameters.</li></ul><p><b>CONTAINERS </b>Time allowed is 2 minutes, set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>12 to 18 containers, of which one is a hot container.</li><li>The containers are to be set in a geometrical configuration. Start line at least 1m away.</li><li>Boundaries should be clearly identifiable.</li><li>Containers are to be random in size and can be plastic or cardboard but must be all the same material - plastic or cardboard. </li><li>When in position containers are to be no higher than 400mm.</li><li>There is to be a minimum distance of 750mm and a maximum distance of 1500mm between </li><li>containers. If the containers are in rows there is to be a minimum distance of 900mm and a </li><li>maximum distance of 1500 between rows.</li><li>There should be additional spare containers, including a spare hot container, in case any need to be replaced.</li><li>The spare additional containers should not be in eyesight of the search area, and the hot container must be separated from the cold containers.</li></ul><p><b>INTERIOR</b> Time allowed is 3 minutes, set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>The area to be searched should be a minimum of 60sqm and no larger than 140sqm, in a single room.</li><li>It can be staged (having additional “stuff” put into the area) or left as you have found it “natural”.</li><li>To calculate the area of the search use the external boundaries of the search area. </li><li>The maximum height for placement of the hide is 650 mm.</li></ul><p><b>E</b><b>XTERIOR</b> Time allowed is 4 minutes, set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>The area to be searched is to be a minimum area of 100sqm and no larger area than 300 sqm.</li><li>It can be staged (having additional “stuff” put into the area) or left as you have found it “natural”.</li><li>To calculate the area of the search use the external boundaries of the search area.</li><li>The maximum height for placement of hide is 650 mm.</li></ul><p><b>VEHICLE</b> Time allowed is 3 minutes set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>2 vehicles as per the SSANZ Regulations definition.</li><li>The first vehicle must be no less than 3m and no more than 10m from the start line.</li><li>Boundaries should be clearly identifiable.</li><li>Maximum height of hide on a vehicle is 650 mm, and it is to be no more than 100 mm deep</li><li>The hide must NOT be placed inside the vehicle.</li><li>There is to be a minimum of 2 metres between the vehicles and between the vehicles and boundaries.</li><li>Suitable hide placements on a vehicle are underneath the towbar, number plates, in the wheel arch or wheel hub. </li><li>When placing a hide on a vehicle, think about placement to minimise dogs having to put their paws on the vehicle to avoid the possibility of the vehicle being scratched. </li></ul><p><b>If a trial certificate needs to be altered for any reason it must be signed by the judge concerned, or a new trial certificate must be completed.</b></p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><b>CHECKLIST</b></p></td><td><p><b>YES</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><ul><li>Have you measured your search area?</li><li>Are there clear parameters for the handler to see what area is in play?</li><li>Have you determined the boundaries of your contamination-free zone?</li></ul></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you measured and checked the height of your hide, plus the depth on the vehicle?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you checked your search area for possible dangers or hazards?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you checked that your hide is not visible to handlers? <br>Do this with a cold hide before placing the hot hide out.</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>REMEMBER TO REMAIN VIGILANT FOR POTENTIAL FAULTS UNTIL A TEAM LEAVES YOUR <br>PREDETERMINED CONTAMINATION FREE ZONE.</b></p></td><td></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Guidelines for Advanced - Trial Checklist","t":"Guidelines for Advanced - Trial Checklist","h":"<p>The purpose of an Advanced trial is to test the team’s proficiency on simple two-hide searches and the ability of the team to work through large cold areas, multiple spaces, more vehicles in different patterns, and more containers, all in a short timeframe while also demonstrating the ability to solve simple scent puzzles.</p><p>The increased challenge at the Advanced level is that the team must source two hides under the pressure of time where the dog is presented with odour from two different locations.</p><p>The team may encounter unproductive spaces, converging odour, and basic elevation challenges.</p><p>Airflow puzzles should be simple as the challenge of two hides increases the difficulty of the search.</p><p>Hides should be placed in a location where odour is accessible to the dog but may test the dog’s skills of working through basic pooling, perimeters, or thresholds (hide placed close to the start line of the search). If a scent puzzle involves converging odour, it must be from two different odours and the hides should be placed at different heights.</p><p>The team should NOT encounter extreme converging odour with hides in very close proximity, deep accessible, or inaccessible hides.</p><p>The team should demonstrate the ability to source two hides and work efficiently through a large search area or multiple search areas.</p><p>Be generous when setting times. It is not a speed test. The search should be achievable by both slow and fast dogs.</p><p>We need to remember that the aim is to test the team’s ability to work together. Odour should be available, accessible, and unambiguous.</p><p>Handlers need to be able to handle their dog in a manner that suits the team to navigate the whole search area, they need to be able to recognise when their dog is encountering odour and how best to allow their dog to work it. Handlers need to be able to recognise the dog’s behaviour in the absence of odour and allow the dog to move on. </p><ul><li>Each search has <b>TWO HIDES</b>: Oil of Clove Bud and/or Sweet Birch or a combo of both.</li><li>Hides should be placed at different height levels within the search area and if setting a converging puzzle, the odours used in each hide should be different (example 1 hide Sweet Birch, the other Clove Bud).  Hides can be set low or high, anywhere <b>in </b>the search area including corners, ground hides or close to the start line. We need to remember the aim is to test the team’s ability not to trick the dog.</li><li>Vessels/hides for odour are to be no larger than 50 ml capacity. The hides must have vent holes.</li><li>The dog should not be able to bite/ remove and/or ingest the vessels, nor should the hides be visible to the handler or the dog. </li><li>The hides must be accessible except for containers, where the nature of containers does not allow for them to be accessible.</li><li>Each element must have a clear start line, and the interior/exterior searches must have clear search parameters.</li><li>Time will NOT be stopped in between alert calls within an element search.</li></ul><p><b>CONTAINERS</b> Time allowed 3 minutes, set by SSANZ regulations.</p><ul><li>18 to 20 containers, set in a geometric shape, of which two will be hot containers.</li><li>The containers of no more than 400mm in height must be random size and made from cardboard, plastic, or metal. There can be a mixture of different types of containers.</li><li>Start line 1m or more from the containers.</li><li>A min. distance of 300mm and a max. distance of 900mm between containers. If containers are in rows there is to be a min. distance of 900mm and a max. distance of 1500mm between rows.</li><li>1 container is to have a non-food distractor in it. It should not move in a manner that if the dog interacts with the container the handler will realise that it is the distraction container.</li></ul><p><b>INTERIOR Time</b> allowed 2-6min in total, exact duration set<b> </b>by the judge. </p><ul><li>The area to be searched should be a minimum of 120sqm and no larger than 400sqm. It can be “staged” or natural.</li><li>Two to three rooms adjacent to or adjoining each other. </li><li>Max height for placement of hides is 900 mm.</li></ul><p><b>EXTERIOR</b> Time allowed 3-5 min; exact duration set by judge.</p><ul><li>Area between 150 – 400 sqm staged or natural.</li><li>Max height for placement of hides is 900 mm.</li></ul><p><b>VEHICLE</b> Time allowed 3 to 4 min; exact duration set by judge. </p><ul><li>3 to 4 vehicles of any sort (check definition in regs). If a trailer is connected to a vehicle, it will be classified as two vehicles. </li><li>Hides must be placed on different vehicles and <b>must not be</b> inside the vehicles.</li><li>Clear minimum 2 metres of access on all sides of the vehicles, unless they are attached to each other. Can be parked in any configuration.  Start line between 3 and 10m from first vehicle.</li><li>Max height of hide on a vehicle is 650 mm, and no more than 100 mm deep.</li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><b>      CHECKLIST</b></p></td><td><p><b>YES</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p> Have you measured out your search area and determined your contamination free zone?</p><p> Are there clear parameters for the handler to see what area is in play? </p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p> Have you set two hides?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p> Have you measured and checked the height of your hides, plus the depth for vehicles?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p> Have you checked your search area for possible dangers or hazards?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p> Have you noted the time you have set for the search on the running order so that this <br> information can be passed onto the Trial Results person?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p> Have you placed your non-food distractor inside a container?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>REMEMBER TO REMAIN VIGILANT FOR POTENTIAL FAULTS UNTIL A TEAM LEAVES YOUR <br>PREDETERMINED CONTAMINATION FREE ZONE.</b></p></td><td></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Guidelines for Excellent - Trial Checklist","t":"Guidelines for Excellent - Trial Checklist","h":"<p>The purpose of an Excellent trial is to test the team’s proficiency in solving a series of simple single and multiple hide scent puzzles in relatively complex set-ups, all within restricted timeframes.</p><p>The increased challenge at Excellent level is that the team must successfully source twelve hides over five element searches while working under the pressure of time and with the possibility of the dog encountering odour from two or three different locations.</p><p>Teams may encounter unproductive spaces, converging odour, and basic elevation challenges.</p><p>Airflow puzzles should be simple as the challenge of multiple hides across five searches increases the difficulty of the trial. </p><p>Hides should be placed in a location where odour is accessible to the dog but may test the dog’s skills of working through basic pooling, perimeters, or thresholds (hide placed close to the start line of the search). </p><p>If a scent puzzle involves converging odour, it must be from two different odours and the hides should be placed at different heights.</p><p>Teams should NOT encounter extreme converging odour with hides in very close proximity, deep accessible, or inaccessible hides.</p><p>Be generous when setting times. It is not a speed test. The search should be achievable by both slow and fast dogs.</p><p>We need to remember that the aim is to test the team’s ability to work together. Odour should be available, accessible, and unambiguous.</p><p>Teams should demonstrate the ability to source one, two or three hides and work efficiently through large or multiple search areas.</p><p>Handlers need to handle their dog to navigate the entire search area in a manner that suits the team, be able to recognise when their dog is encountering odour and know how best to allow their dog to work it. Handlers need to recognise the dog’s behaviour in the absence of odour and allow the dog to move on. </p><ul><li>There are a total of twelve hides across five searches. </li><li>Hides may comprise Oil of Clove Bud, Sweet Birch or Cypress, or a combination of any two.</li><li>Multiple hides should be placed at different heights within the search area and if setting a converging puzzle, the odours used in each hide should be different (example one hide Sweet Birch, the other Clove Bud).  </li><li>Hides can be set low or high, anywhere in the search area including corners, ground hides or close to the start line. We need to remember the aim is to test the team’s ability not to trick the dog.</li><li>Vessels/hides for odour are to be no larger than 50 ml capacity and must have vent holes.</li><li>The dog should not be able to bite, remove and/or ingest a vessel, nor should a hide be visible to the handler or the dog. </li><li>All hides must be accessible except for containers, where the nature of containers does not allow them to be accessible.</li><li>Each element must have a clear start line and search parameters.</li><li>Time will NOT be stopped in between alert calls within an element search.</li></ul><p><b>CONTAINERS</b> Time allowed 3-4 minutes; exact duration being set by the judge.</p><ul><li>18 to 22 containers, set in a geometric shape.</li><li>Containers must be random in size and of different materials from cardboard, plastic, or metal. </li><li>Containers shall be no more than 400mm in height.</li><li>Start line 1m or more from the containers and a min. distance of 300mm and a max. distance of 900mm between containers. If containers are in rows there is to be a min. distance of 900mm and a max. distance of 1500mm between rows.</li><li>There are to be 3 distractions inside 3 containers; 2 non-food distractions and one food distraction. Distractions should not move in a manner that if the dog interacts with the container the handler will realise it is a distraction.</li></ul><p><b>INTERIOR Time</b> allowed 3-6min in total; exact duration being set by the judge.</p><ul><li>The area to be searched should be a minimum of 120sqm and no larger than 400sqm. It can be “staged” or natural.</li><li>Two to four rooms adjacent to or adjoining each other. </li><li>Max height for placement of hides is 1100 mm.</li></ul><p><b>EXTERIOR</b> Time allowed 3-6 min; exact duration being set by the judge.</p><ul><li>Area between 150 – 400 sqm staged or natural.</li><li>Max height for placement of hides is 1100 mm.</li></ul><p><b>VEHICLE</b> Time allowed 3-4 min; exact duration being set by the judge.</p><ul><li>3 to 4 vehicles (see regs). If a trailer is connected to a vehicle, it will be classed as two vehicles. </li><li>Hides must be placed on different vehicles and <b>must not be</b> inside the vehicles.</li><li>Clear minimum 2 metres of access on all sides of the vehicles, unless they are attached to each other. Can be parked in any configuration.  </li><li>Start line between 3 and 10m from first vehicle.</li><li>Max height of hide on a private passenger vehicle is 650 mm and on other vehicles is 1000mm. </li><li>Hides may be no more than 150 mm deep. </li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><b>      CHECKLIST</b></p></td><td><p><b>YES</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you measured out your search area and determined your contamination free zone?</p><p> Are there clear parameters for the handler to see what area is in play? </p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you set the allotted number of hides?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you double checked the height of your hides, &amp; the depth on vehicles?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you checked your search area for possible dangers or hazards?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you set a time for the search &amp; marked it on the running order?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Have you placed your distractors in the containers?</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>REMEMBER TO REMAIN VIGILANT FOR POTENTIAL FAULTS UNTIL A TEAM LEAVES YOUR <br>PREDETERMINED CONTAMINATION FREE ZONE.</b></p></td><td></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Novice - Sample Container Layouts","t":"Novice - Sample Container Layouts","h":"<p>Below are samples of different geometric shapes that might be suitable at Novice level.</p><ul><li>Remember that this is entry level, with the inherent challenge being that the odour source is inaccessible.</li><li>Container number, type, size, and spacing must all comply with current regulations.</li><li>Images are indicative only, and not to scale.</li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p>  <img data-key=\"kix.x5xv72boakoy\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"166\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.dmhcz2ro3v\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"170\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.g5eli315brak\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"144\" height=\"134\"></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.tzmb18srhl10\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"156\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.uo2mf8ugq7l\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"171\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.pm3rt0n8yqcw\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"218\" height=\"109\"></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.3tmrjxilvnpd\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"170\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Intermediate - Sample Container Layouts","t":"Intermediate - Sample Container Layouts","h":"<p>Below are samples of different geometric shapes that might be suitable at Intermediate level.</p><ul><li>Layouts may be similar to Novice, plus other relatively simple layouts, samples below.</li><li>Remember, some complexity at Intermediate already arises from there being mixed containers, and no option for a false alert.</li><li>Container number, type, size, and spacing must all comply with current regulations.</li><li>Images are indicative only, and not to scale.</li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.g1lkscxssvqz\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"192\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.j69hnx5b2i7z\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"151\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.5a8pyu8x3hix\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"134\" height=\"134\"></p></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Advanced - Sample Container Layouts","t":"Advanced - Sample Container Layouts","h":"<p>Below are samples of different geometric shapes that might be suitable at Advanced level.</p><ul><li>Layouts may be similar to previous levels, or form more complex shapes, examples below.</li><li>Remember that complexity already exists with the increased number of containers and hides.</li><li>Container number, type, size, and spacing must all comply with current regulations.</li><li>Images are indicative only, and not to scale.</li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.avp60thetjg4\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"97\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p> <img data-key=\"kix.tzkm5ma4mey1\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"138\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.tzcx4yoagg0i\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"132\" height=\"134\"></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.yth6abmexp0s\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"136\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.voqi0w4rr85g\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"133\" height=\"134\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.gx4ex7zcg0sr\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"92\" height=\"134\"></p></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Excellent - Sample Container Layouts","t":"Excellent - Sample Container Layouts","h":"<p>Below are samples of different geometric shapes that might be suitable at Excellent level.</p><ul><li>Layouts may be similar to previous levels, or form more complex shapes, examples below.</li><li>Remember that complexity already exists with the increased number of containers, hides and the presence of distractors.</li><li>Container number, type, size, and spacing must all comply with current regulations.</li><li>Images are indicative only, and not to scale.</li></ul><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.yhdfd4rasc5p\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"148\" height=\"157\"></p></td><td><p> <img data-key=\"kix.pqs6nivrkptj\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"140\" height=\"133\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.8gw12tlou0d\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"155\" height=\"155\"></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.yek7s5cmu59c\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"134\" height=\"122\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.2tjwghv90x40\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"153\" height=\"141\"></p></td><td><p><img data-key=\"kix.e7mndw3q2e5m\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"90\" height=\"136\"></p></td></tr></table></div>"},{"g":"Distractions","t":"Distractions","h":"<p>Container searches at the Advanced level and above will require one or more distractions. It is generally up to the judge to provide these. If you want the organisation to source anything for you, please arrange this with them in advance.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><p><b>LEVEL</b></p></td><td><p><b>DISTRACTIONS</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Advanced</p></td><td><p>One non-food item<br>(any item that is not intended for consumption by a person or dog)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Excellent</p></td><td><p>Two non-food items &amp; one food item</p><p>(any item that a dog or person can safely eat)</p></td></tr></table></div><p>Examples of items not permitted as distractions are Kong, gun oil, droppings, poisons, any item containing essential oils, any item that is perishable, rotten, dead (e.g. animal, birds, fish), anything toxic to a dog, any highly volatile substances. If you are unsure if it qualifies, leave it out.</p>"},{"g":"Judges Kit","t":"Judges Kit","h":"<p>The content of your judge’s kit is a matter of personal choice. </p><p>Organisations will generally provide the following however some judges opt to bring their own:</p><ul><li>Scented Q-tips</li><li>A selection of different hide vessels</li><li>Fixings e.g. sticky dots, blue-tac and/or velcro dots</li><li>Scissors and tweezers</li><li>Clipboard (with running order and judging sheets) &amp; pens</li><li>Stop watch</li><li>Tape measure</li><li>Gloves</li><li>A container for disposal of anything that is “hot”</li><li>Cones - boundary and start cones. Hopefully also some contrasting cones so that any fouling or contamination can be easily identified.</li></ul><p>Your personal items:</p><ul><li>All weather gear including sunscreen</li><li>Mobile device (on silent) with a link to this document and the regulations. Some people prefer hard copies but these need to be up to date. Your phone will also be needed to record your debrief video</li><li>Personal refreshments - drink bottle and/or keep cup and snacks </li><li>First aid items</li></ul><p>Additional items that some judges have in their kits are:</p><ul><li>Marker pen / tape / post it notes to clearly identify the hot containers when packing up</li><li>A wide, flat head screwdriver to help remove those pesky container lids</li><li>Pliers to get to those hard to reach places for hide placement and removal</li><li>A selection of tapes - gaffer, double sided, painters tape</li><li>A small, stiff bristled brush to clean dust and grime off a surface before attaching the hide</li><li>Some form of smoke machine (ie Cirrus Wind Indicator) or matches for information on odour movement <u>after</u> the search is completed</li><li>Rope, chalk and/or golf tees to mark out container locations</li><li>Paper towels, wet wipes (odourless) or other cleaning cloths</li><li>Poo bags which can double as rubbish bags if you need to clear the search area.</li></ul><p><b>IMPORTANT</b><br>If you do take your own Q-tips they must be prepared according to the regulations. Also any hide vessels that you supply should be new and not previously used either in training or for a trial.</p>"},{"g":"Odour Preparation","t":"Odour Preparation","h":"<p>Our regulations read as follows:</p><p><i>8.1   Three to five drops of a single essential oil are to be placed on a cotton make up remover<br>pad at the base of a sealable glass jar. Q-tips/cotton buds are then added to the jar. Note that <br>Q-tips/cotton buds must not have plastic handles. The jar is then sealed with an airtight lid to allow the odour to infuse. </i></p><p><i>Q-tips must be prepared a minimum of 24 and a maximum of 72 hours before use at a trial.</i></p><p><i>8.2   Two Q‑tips must be placed inside the hide vessel. </i></p><p><i>8.3   At Advanced level and above, combination hides may be used by placing two different odour infused Q-tips in the same vessel.</i></p><p>Ana Cilursu has kindly permitted us to share a video. Click on the image below to access it. We would like to acknowledge both Ana and Stacy Barnett for allowing us to share. Ana is teaching assistant for Stacy’s online Nosework classes with Fenzi Dog Sports Academy.</p><p><img data-key=\"kix.vu2gwxjurpxw\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"492\" height=\"306\"></p><p>The video shows how odour should be prepared for SSANZ trials.  It demonstrates how to prepare Q-tips safely and without contaminating your work area. </p><p>Place at least as many Q-tips as you will need for an upcoming trial (assuming two Q-tips per hide) then add some extras just in case. </p><p>Note - judges do not have to supply odour for trials, but many prefer to.</p>"},{"g":"Odour Preparation","t":"Buried Hides","h":"<p>Q-tips for Buried Hides are prepared with much stronger odour because it has to move through the substrate (sand or water) to become available to the dog. </p><p><i>7.1 Specific for Buried Hides: Q-tips (must have paper shafts) are to be prepared with one drop of essential oil applied directly to the Q-tip no less than 24 and no more than 72 hours before use and then stored in a glass jar with an airtight lid before being used. <br><br>7.2 Two Q-tips are to be used in each container. </i></p><p>Note that for sand the scent vessel must be a round stainless steel mesh tea ball/tea infuser with a maximum diameter of 50mm, and for water the Q-tips must have paper shafts, not bamboo. </p>"},{"g":"Odour Preparation","t":"Coloured Q-Tips","h":"<p>Some member organisations are using coloured Q-tips to distinguish between odours.  Be aware that the dyes used may interact with the odour, in the same way as odour and plastic hide vessels will have a chemical reaction. Should you wish to use coloured tips, below is the colour matching that we recommend.  </p><p>As several of our judges also judge for other codes, we have included an entire list of current odours being used in NZ.</p><p><b>Clove ( SSANZ, SSG, NNDT) – Green  </b></p><p><b>Sweet Birch (SSANZ, SSG, NDDT) – Pink </b></p><p><b>Cedarwood Atlas (DNZ, SSG, NDDT) – Blue </b></p><p><b>Lemongrass (DNZ, SSG, NDDT) – Yellow </b></p><p><b>Cypress (SSANZ) – White</b></p><p><b>Bergamont (DNZ, SSG, NDDT) – Black</b></p><p><b>Clary-Sage (DNZ) – Purple</b></p><p><b>Key</b></p><p>SSANZ = Scent Sport Association NZ</p><p>SSG = Scent Snout Games</p><p>DNZ = Dogs New Zealand</p><p>NDDT = Noseworx Detector Dog Trials</p>"},{"g":"Learning Resources","t":"Learning Resources","h":"<p><img data-key=\"kix.8ueqlrwyli3i\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"327\" height=\"703\"></p>"},{"g":"Learning Resources","t":"Podcasts","h":"<p><b>Alert!</b> A scentwork podcast  - interviews with judges and more</p><p><b>Find Your Fix</b> - Sport Dog Scent Detection Podcast - Jill Kovacevich and Alecs Woodroffe - who are both judges and handlers talk about all things related to our sport.  There are some great insights about judging.</p>"},{"g":"Accessing Reference Documents from your Phone","t":"Accessing Reference Documents from your Phone","h":"<p>To ensure that you have access to the latest version of SSANZ documents when at a trial, it is advisable to view them on a digital device. </p><p>If you're creating shortcuts for several documents (such as your SSANZ documents), you can place them together in a folder on your Home Screen and even customise each icon with a different colour or symbol so they're easy to recognise at a glance.</p><p>Make sure that your settings enable these to be viewed off-line in case of poor network at the trial location.</p><p>On the following pages you will find 2 handy guides prepared by Jo Cheramie for either Android or iPhone.</p><p><img data-key=\"kix.t6z9ekr88yt3\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"615\" height=\"872\"></p><p><img data-key=\"kix.pdq1snusetlh\" alt=\"Picture from Beyond the Sniff\" width=\"606\" height=\"856\"></p>"}],"images":{}}};
